// Project data, extracted from the original index.html.
// Single source of truth for the tile grid and the write up overlay.
const PROJECTS=[{"title": "Wikipedia Edit Stream: Streaming Algorithms with Their Error Measured Live", "meta": "Streaming Algorithms &middot; Live Data &middot; Oct 2026", "badge": "HyperLogLog 986.9 vs exact 983 (+0.4%) &middot; 0 Space-Saving violations &middot; live SSE, about 30 events/s", "tags": ["JavaScript", "Server Sent Events", "HyperLogLog", "Count-Min Sketch", "Space-Saving", "MurmurHash3", "Stream Processing", "Canvas", "node:test"], "body": "<div class=\"metric\">9,909 real edits in a 297 second run (Oct 8 2026) &nbsp;|&nbsp; HyperLogLog 986.9 vs 983 exact distinct editors (+0.4%, one sigma bound 1.6%) &nbsp;|&nbsp; Space-Saving: 0 bound violations &nbsp;|&nbsp; Count-Min: 12 of 12 within its error bound &nbsp;|&nbsp; 24 tests</div>\n<figure class=\"project-figure\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" src=\"images/live_wiki.jpg\" alt=\"The edit river with a dot for each real edit, in a row for its wiki, blue for people and orange for bots, drifting left as it ages\"><figcaption>The edit river on October 8, 2026. Each dot is one real edit in a row for its wiki, blue for people and orange for bots, drifting left as it ages; rings are new pages.</figcaption></figure>\n<h4>The Problem</h4>\n<p>Streaming sketches are usually taught with their error bounds and then trusted. HyperLogLog promises a distinct count within about 1.6% from 4 KB of state. Count-Min promises it never undercounts, and rarely overcounts by more than a small, known share of all events (&epsilon;N, with probability 1 &minus; &delta;). Space-Saving promises that every page popular enough to matter (above N/k edits, for k counters) is in its table, with a known error on every count. I wanted to watch those promises hold on real traffic instead of taking them on faith, so this dashboard runs each sketch beside an exact counter on the same events and plots the difference as it happens.</p>\n<h4>The Data</h4>\n<p>Wikimedia publishes every change to every one of its wikis as a server sent events stream. The browser connects to it directly, with no server of mine in between. In my runs it carried 26 to 36 events a second across about 100 wikis, roughly a quarter of them from bots. If the connection drops, the browser reconnects with Last-Event-ID, which carries the stream's Kafka offsets, so it resumes where it stopped; events are deduplicated on their id in case the resume overlaps.</p>\n<h4>The Sketches</h4>\n<p><strong>HyperLogLog</strong> hashes each editor name with MurmurHash3, checked against published test vectors and SMHasher's verification value, into 4,096 one byte registers. While there are fewer than about 10,000 distinct editors it estimates from the share of empty registers (linear counting), and above that it switches to the harmonic mean estimator from the original paper. <strong>Count-Min</strong> counts events per wiki in 4 rows of 1,024 columns with the standard update, so the textbook bound applies as stated. <strong>Space-Saving</strong> tracks the most edited pages with 200 counters, each carrying its own error term, so every row shows a guaranteed lower bound and an upper bound, with the exact count drawn as a tick between them.</p>\n<h4>Measured on Real Data</h4>\n<p>A node script ran the same sketch code against the live stream for 297 seconds on October 8, 2026: 9,909 events at 33.3 a second. HyperLogLog estimated 986.9 distinct editors against an exact 983, an error of +0.4% against a one sigma bound of 1.6%. Two shorter runs gave &minus;0.3% and &minus;1.2%. Space-Saving showed no violations of its bounds, and Count-Min kept all 12 of the busiest wikis within &epsilon;N with no undercounts. The run logs are in the repository next to the code.</p>\n<h4>Engineering Decisions</h4>\n<p><strong>Binning by event time.</strong> Binning throughput by arrival flagged delivery clumps as bursts: 3 and 2 false bursts in two of the runs, against none when the same rule ran on event time bins. The chart bins by event time and closes a second 2 seconds after the newest event passes it, and the footer still reports what the arrival rule would have flagged.</p>\n<p><strong>A rate bug in the feed monitor.</strong> The status pill read 23 messages a second while a separate counter saw 33, because events arriving less than a millisecond apart carried almost no weight in the smoothed rate. Pooling arrivals into 50 ms windows fixed it, in the shared code all four dashboards use.</p>\n<p><strong>Privacy.</strong> Editor names and IP addresses are hashed for counting and never displayed, and titles of user pages are hidden.</p>\n<h4>Limitations</h4>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Count-Min barely collides here.</strong> About 100 wikis in 1,024 columns rarely share a cell, so the bound is shown but hardly stressed. The card says so beside the number.</li>\n  <li><strong>A short visit exercises only the small range path of HyperLogLog.</strong> It switches to the raw estimator at about 10,240 distinct editors, which takes a long session to reach.</li>\n  <li><strong>Exact counting is affordable at this rate.</strong> That is what makes the comparison possible. The sketches earn their keep at volumes where it is not.</li>\n  <li><strong>The resume path is lightly tested.</strong> Wikimedia dropped the stream once during browser testing, and the page reconnected with Last-Event-ID without a duplicate, but no logged run has exercised it.</li>\n</ul>", "simple": "<h4>What I Built</h4>\n<p>Wikipedia and its sister sites publish every edit as it happens, as a live public feed. In my runs it carried 26 to 36 edits a second across about 100 wikis. I built a web page that connects to that feed straight from the browser and counts the edits two ways at once: exactly, and with three memory saving shortcuts called sketches.</p>\n<p>A sketch keeps a small summary instead of every item and gives up a little accuracy in return. Each one comes with a mathematical promise about how far off it can be, and they are usually taught with that promise and then trusted. I wanted to watch the promises hold on real traffic, so the page runs each sketch next to an exact count of the same edits and plots the gap as it happens.</p>\n<h4>The Three Shortcuts</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Counting distinct editors.</strong> HyperLogLog estimates how many different people and bots have edited, from a tiny fixed amount of memory. Its typical error is about 1.6%.</li>\n<li><strong>Edits per wiki.</strong> Count-Min never undercounts, and rarely overcounts by more than a small, known share of all edits.</li>\n<li><strong>The most edited pages.</strong> Space-Saving keeps a short list of the busiest pages, with a guaranteed lowest and highest possible count for each one.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Editor names and IP addresses are scrambled before counting and never shown.</p>\n<h4>What I Found</h4>\n<p>In one logged run of 297 seconds on October 8, 2026, HyperLogLog estimated 986.9 distinct editors against an exact count of 983, off by 0.4%. Two shorter runs came in slightly low, by 0.3% and 1.2%. In the logged run, Space-Saving never broke its bounds, and Count-Min kept all 12 of the busiest wikis inside its promised error, with no undercounts.</p>\n<p>I also found that counting edits by when they arrived made the page report bursts of activity that were only delivery clumps in the feed. Counting them by when the edit was made removed those false bursts, so the chart uses that.</p>\n<h4>What It Does Not Show</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Count-Min is barely stressed.</strong> About 100 wikis spread over its 1,024 slots rarely land in the same one, so its promise is shown but hardly tested.</li>\n<li><strong>A short visit tests only one mode of HyperLogLog.</strong> It switches methods at about 10,240 distinct editors, which takes a long session to reach.</li>\n<li><strong>Exact counting is cheap at this rate.</strong> That is what makes the comparison possible. The shortcuts pay off at volumes where exact counting is not affordable.</li>\n<li><strong>Reconnecting is lightly tested.</strong> The feed dropped once during testing and the page picked up where it left off without a duplicate, but no logged run covers it.</li>\n</ul>", "flow": [{"k": "start", "t": "Do streaming sketches keep their published error bounds on real traffic?", "d": "HyperLogLog, Count-Min and Space-Saving are usually taught with their bounds and then trusted. The dashboard runs each beside an exact counter on the same events and plots the difference live."}, {"k": "data", "t": "Wikimedia recent changes, a server sent events stream", "d": "Every change to every Wikimedia wiki, read by the browser with no server in between. 26 to 36 events a second across about 100 wikis, roughly a quarter from bots."}, {"k": "check", "t": "Did the connection drop?", "d": "The stream can disconnect mid session.", "yes": "The browser reconnects with Last-Event-ID, which carries the stream's Kafka offsets, and resumes where it stopped. Events are deduplicated on id in case the resume overlaps.", "no": "Events flow straight on to the counters."}, {"k": "step", "t": "Privacy handling", "d": "Editor names and IP addresses are hashed for counting and never displayed, and titles of user pages are hidden."}, {"k": "split", "t": "Three sketches, each beside an exact counter", "lanes": [[{"k": "step", "t": "HyperLogLog on editor names", "d": "MurmurHash3, checked against published test vectors and SMHasher's verification value, into 4,096 one byte registers (4 KB of state). The promise: a distinct count within about 1.6%."}, {"k": "check", "t": "Fewer than about 10,000 distinct editors so far?", "yes": "Estimate from the share of empty registers (linear counting).", "no": "Switch to the harmonic mean estimator from the original paper."}], [{"k": "step", "t": "Count-Min on events per wiki", "d": "4 rows of 1,024 columns with the standard update, so the textbook bound applies: never undercounts, rarely overcounts by more than &epsilon;N, with probability 1 &minus; &delta;."}], [{"k": "step", "t": "Space-Saving on the most edited pages", "d": "200 counters, each carrying its own error term, so every row shows a guaranteed lower and upper bound, with the exact count drawn as a tick between them."}]]}, {"k": "step", "t": "Logged run: 297 seconds on October 8, 2026", "d": "A node script ran the same sketch code against the live stream: 9,909 events at 33.3 a second. The run logs sit in the repository next to the code, with 24 tests."}, {"k": "result", "t": "HyperLogLog: 986.9 estimated against 983 exact, +0.4%", "d": "Against a one sigma bound of 1.6%, in that single 297 second run. Two shorter runs gave &minus;0.3% and &minus;1.2%."}, {"k": "check", "t": "Does Space-Saving break its bounds?", "d": "Each tracked page's exact count is compared with its lower and upper bound.", "yes": "The guarantee would be broken on real traffic.", "no": "0 bound violations in the logged run."}, {"k": "check", "t": "Do the busiest wikis stay within &epsilon;N?", "d": "Count-Min's estimate for each of the 12 busiest wikis against its exact count.", "yes": "All 12 of 12 within &epsilon;N, with no undercounts, in the logged run.", "no": "An undercount or an overshoot past &epsilon;N would break the textbook bound."}, {"k": "check", "t": "Does binning throughput by arrival time flag false bursts?", "d": "The same burst rule run on arrival time bins and on event time bins.", "yes": "It flagged delivery clumps as bursts: 3 and 2 false bursts in two runs, against none on event time. The chart bins by event time, closing a second 2 seconds after the newest event passes it.", "no": "Either binning would do."}, {"k": "step", "t": "Rate bug fixed in the shared feed monitor", "d": "The status pill read 23 messages a second while a separate counter saw 33, because events arriving under a millisecond apart barely moved the smoothed rate. Pooling arrivals into 50 ms windows fixed it for all four dashboards."}, {"k": "limit", "t": "Count-Min barely collides here", "d": "About 100 wikis in 1,024 columns rarely share a cell, so its bound is shown but hardly stressed. The card says so beside the number."}, {"k": "limit", "t": "A short visit uses only HyperLogLog's small range path", "d": "It switches to the raw estimator at about 10,240 distinct editors, which takes a long session to reach."}, {"k": "limit", "t": "Exact counting is affordable at this rate", "d": "That is what makes the comparison possible. The sketches earn their keep at volumes where it is not."}, {"k": "limit", "t": "The resume path is lightly tested", "d": "The stream dropped once during browser testing and the page reconnected without a duplicate, but no logged run has exercised it."}, {"k": "end", "t": "How to read it", "d": "On one logged run of real edits, all three sketches stayed inside their published bounds. Count-Min barely collides at this volume, and a short session does not reach HyperLogLog's large range estimator, so neither is stressed."}]}, {"title": "Order Book Microstructure: A Checksum Verified Live Book with Order Flow Analytics", "meta": "Market Microstructure &middot; Live Data &middot; Oct 2026", "badge": "46,892 of 46,892 checksums verified &middot; OFI R&sup2; 0.54 at 1 s &middot; live WebSocket, depth 100", "tags": ["JavaScript", "WebSocket", "Order Book", "CRC32", "Order Flow Imbalance", "OLS Regression", "Realized Volatility", "Canvas Heatmap", "node:test"], "body": "<div class=\"metric\">330 second run on BTC/USD (Oct 8 2026): 46,892 of 46,892 book checksums verified, 0 resyncs &nbsp;|&nbsp; OFI regression at 1 s: &beta; = 1.10 USD per BTC, R&sup2; = 0.54, n = 300 &nbsp;|&nbsp; lag p50 89 ms &nbsp;|&nbsp; 16 tests</div>\n<figure class=\"project-figure\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" src=\"images/live_book.jpg\" alt=\"Liquidity heatmap of the BTC/USD order book with the best bid and ask lines and trades drawn as bubbles\"><figcaption>The BTC/USD book on October 8, 2026. Darker blue means more bitcoin waiting at that price; the best bid and ask run across it and every trade is a bubble sized by amount.</figcaption></figure>\n<h4>The Problem</h4>\n<p>Every trading system starts from a local copy of the order book, built from a snapshot and a stream of deltas, and the classic failure is silent drift. Miss or misapply one update and every number computed from the book is quietly wrong. Kraken publishes a checksum (CRC32) of the top of its book with every update, so drift does not have to be silent. This dashboard rebuilds the book in the browser, verifies it against that checksum on every message, resyncs automatically on a mismatch, and only then computes microstructure statistics from it.</p>\n<h4>The Book</h4>\n<p>The page subscribes to Kraken's public WebSocket at a depth of 100 levels and keeps each side as a sorted array with binary search updates. After each update it truncates both sides back to the subscribed depth, because the exchange stops sending changes for levels that fall out of range. Price and size precision are read from the exchange's instrument channel rather than hardcoded. The checksum is computed over formatted strings: printing 0.0157 instead of 0.01570000 changes the string and fails every check.</p>\n<h4>What It Computes</h4>\n<p><strong>Order flow imbalance</strong> measures whether buyers or sellers are pressing harder at the best prices. Following Cont, Kukanov and Stoikov (2014), each change at the top of the book adds or subtracts size, summed per second, and the page fits a straight line between that and the price change over the same second (ordinary least squares), live, with the slope's standard error. <strong>Microprice</strong> weights the midpoint by how much size waits on each side. <strong>Realized volatility</strong> is computed from mid returns at both 1 second and 10 second sampling over the same five minutes, annualized. A liquidity heatmap shows resting size by price over the last four minutes with every trade drawn over it, painted from raw pixel buffers so it keeps up with the feed.</p>\n<h4>Measured on Real Data</h4>\n<p>A node script ran the same book code against the live feed for 330 seconds on October 8, 2026: 47,595 messages, of which 46,891 were book updates, about 142 a second. All 46,892 checksums (the snapshot and every update) matched, with no resyncs. The OFI regression over the last 300 seconds gave a slope of 1.10 USD per BTC of net flow, standard error 0.059, with R&sup2; = 0.54. An earlier 330 second run verified 44,534 of 44,534, and a run switching between BTC, ETH and SOL verified 10,214 of 10,214. To exercise the resync path, a deliberately corrupted update failed its checksum and the book recovered from a fresh snapshot; that case is also in the test suite.</p>\n<h4>A Result That Went the Other Way</h4>\n<p>Microstructure noise usually makes finely sampled realized volatility read higher than coarsely sampled. In both long runs it went the other way: 27.0% annualized at 1 second against 31.9% at 10 seconds in the final run. The 10 second figure rests on only 30 returns, so its sampling error is about 13%, and the page compares the gap with that error instead of always calling it noise. It labels the gap as within sampling error, as noise, or as prices moving in short runs, depending on what the numbers support.</p>\n<h4>Limitations</h4>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>One venue, 100 levels.</strong> The book is Kraken's alone, and liquidity beyond the subscribed depth is invisible.</li>\n  <li><strong>Taker side as reported.</strong> Trade direction is the exchange's label, not inferred.</li>\n  <li><strong>Clock offset in latency.</strong> Lag is arrival time minus the exchange timestamp, so it includes any offset between the two clocks.</li>\n  <li><strong>Background tabs.</strong> Browsers throttle timers in hidden tabs, so heatmap columns sampled while the tab is hidden are sparse.</li>\n  <li><strong>Not investment advice.</strong> This is analytics on public market data.</li>\n</ul>", "simple": "<h4>What I Built</h4>\n<p>An order book is an exchange's running list of everyone waiting to buy or sell, and at what price. I built a web page that keeps a live copy of the bitcoin to US dollar order book on Kraken, a crypto exchange, from its public feed. The page starts from a full snapshot of the book and then applies each small change the exchange sends, more than a hundred a second.</p>\n<p>The classic way this goes wrong is quiet drift: miss or misapply one change, and every number worked out from the copy is wrong with nothing to warn you. Kraken sends a short fingerprint of the top of its book (a checksum) with every update. After every change, my page computes the same fingerprint from its own copy and compares the two. If they differ, it throws the copy away and starts again from a fresh snapshot.</p>\n<h4>What It Calculates</h4>\n<p>Once the copy is known to be right, the page works out a few standard trading statistics as the data arrives:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Order flow imbalance:</strong> whether buyers or sellers are pressing harder at the best prices each second, and how closely that lines up with the price move in the same second.</li>\n<li><strong>Microprice:</strong> the midpoint between the best buying and selling prices, adjusted for how much is waiting on each side.</li>\n<li><strong>Volatility:</strong> how much the price is jumping around, measured in 1 second and 10 second steps.</li>\n<li><strong>A heat map</strong> of how much bitcoin is waiting at each price over the last four minutes, with every trade drawn on top.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>What I Found</h4>\n<p>In one 330 second run on October 8, 2026, all 46,892 fingerprints matched, the snapshot and every update, so the page never had to start over. Two other runs also matched every time. To check the recovery, I fed it a deliberately broken update: the check caught it and the book rebuilt itself from a fresh snapshot.</p>\n<p>In that same run, buying and selling pressure lined up with the price move with a fit score of 0.54, where 1 would mean it accounted for every move.</p>\n<p>One result went against the usual pattern. Volatility measured in short steps normally reads higher than in long steps, because of small jitters in the quoted price. In both long runs it read lower. The 10 second figure rests on only 30 price changes, though, so the page compares the gap with its own sampling error before labelling it.</p>\n<h4>What It Does Not Show</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>One exchange, 100 price levels.</strong> The book is Kraken's alone, and anything deeper than the levels it subscribes to is invisible.</li>\n<li><strong>Trade direction is the exchange's label,</strong> not something the page works out itself.</li>\n<li><strong>Delay figures include clock differences</strong> between the exchange and the viewer's computer.</li>\n<li><strong>It is not investment advice.</strong> It is analysis of public market data.</li>\n</ul>", "flow": [{"k": "start", "t": "Can a browser keep a verified live order book and compute statistics from it?", "d": "A local book built from a snapshot plus deltas fails by silent drift: one missed update and every number is quietly wrong. Kraken publishes a CRC32 checksum of its top of book with every update, so drift can be caught."}, {"k": "data", "t": "Kraken public WebSocket, BTC/USD at depth 100", "d": "A snapshot followed by a stream of deltas. Price and size precision come from the exchange's instrument channel rather than being hardcoded."}, {"k": "step", "t": "Maintain the book as two sorted arrays", "d": "Binary search updates on each side, truncated back to the subscribed depth after every update, because the exchange stops sending changes for levels that fall out of range."}, {"k": "check", "t": "Does the local CRC32 match the exchange checksum?", "d": "Run on every message. The checksum is over formatted strings, so printing 0.0157 instead of 0.01570000 would fail every check.", "yes": "The book is trusted and statistics are computed from it.", "no": "The page resyncs automatically from a fresh snapshot."}, {"k": "split", "t": "Statistics computed on the verified book", "lanes": [[{"k": "step", "t": "Order flow imbalance (Cont, Kukanov and Stoikov 2014)", "d": "Size changes at the best prices summed per second, then an ordinary least squares line fitted live against the same second's price change, with the slope's standard error."}], [{"k": "step", "t": "Microprice", "d": "The midpoint weighted by how much size waits on each side."}], [{"k": "step", "t": "Realized volatility at 1 s and 10 s", "d": "From mid returns at both samplings over the same five minutes, annualized."}], [{"k": "step", "t": "Liquidity heatmap", "d": "Resting size by price over the last four minutes with every trade drawn over it, painted from raw pixel buffers so it keeps up with the feed."}]]}, {"k": "step", "t": "Logged run: 330 seconds on October 8, 2026", "d": "A node script ran the same book code against the live feed: 47,595 messages, 46,891 of them book updates, about 142 a second."}, {"k": "result", "t": "46,892 of 46,892 checksums verified, 0 resyncs", "d": "The snapshot and every update in that run. An earlier 330 second run verified 44,534 of 44,534, and a run switching between BTC, ETH and SOL verified 10,214 of 10,214."}, {"k": "check", "t": "Does the resync path recover from a corrupted update?", "d": "A deliberately corrupted update is fed in. The same case is one of the 16 tests.", "yes": "It failed its checksum and the book recovered from a fresh snapshot.", "no": "The book would drift silently from that update on."}, {"k": "result", "t": "OFI at 1 s: slope 1.10 USD per BTC, R&sup2; = 0.54", "d": "Over the last 300 seconds of the logged run (n = 300), standard error 0.059."}, {"k": "check", "t": "Does 1 second volatility read higher than 10 second, as noise predicts?", "d": "Microstructure noise usually makes finely sampled realized volatility read higher than coarsely sampled.", "yes": "The usual pattern would hold.", "no": "In both long runs it went the other way: 27.0% annualized at 1 second against 31.9% at 10 seconds in the final run."}, {"k": "limit", "t": "The 10 second figure rests on only 30 returns", "d": "Its sampling error is about 13%. The page compares the gap with that error and labels it as within sampling error, as noise, or as prices moving in short runs."}, {"k": "result", "t": "Feed lag p50 of 89 ms", "d": "Lag is arrival time minus the exchange timestamp, measured in the logged run."}, {"k": "limit", "t": "Clock offset in latency", "d": "Because lag subtracts the exchange timestamp, it includes any offset between the two clocks."}, {"k": "limit", "t": "One venue, 100 levels", "d": "The book is Kraken's alone, and liquidity beyond the subscribed depth is invisible."}, {"k": "limit", "t": "Taker side as reported", "d": "Trade direction is the exchange's label, not inferred."}, {"k": "limit", "t": "Background tabs", "d": "Browsers throttle timers in hidden tabs, so heatmap columns sampled while the tab is hidden are sparse."}, {"k": "end", "t": "How to read it", "d": "Across three logged runs every checksum matched, so the statistics sit on a book known to be correct. Each figure comes from short runs on one venue, and none of it is investment advice."}]}, {"title": "Live Earthquake Statistics: Gutenberg-Richter, Omori, and a Catalog That Revises Itself", "meta": "Statistical Seismology &middot; Live Data &middot; Oct 2026", "badge": "California b = 0.73 [0.66, 0.85] &middot; 7 catalog revisions caught in 45 min &middot; live USGS polling", "tags": ["JavaScript", "Statistical Seismology", "Maximum Likelihood", "Bootstrap", "Gutenberg-Richter", "Omori-Utsu", "Revision Tracking", "d3-geo", "node:test"], "body": "<div class=\"metric\">1,949 events in the week feed (USGS, Oct 8 2026) &nbsp;|&nbsp; California b = 0.73 &plusmn; 0.03, bootstrap [0.66, 0.85] &nbsp;|&nbsp; 45 min of polling: 9 new events, 7 revisions, mean magnitude change 0.19 &nbsp;|&nbsp; 27 tests</div>\n<figure class=\"project-figure\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" src=\"images/live_quakes.jpg\" alt=\"World map of the week's earthquakes colored by depth, with ripples around the last hour's events\"><figcaption>The week's earthquakes on October 8, 2026, colored by depth, with rings rippling out from the last hour's events and the latest M 2.5 or larger called out.</figcaption></figure>\n<h4>The Problem</h4>\n<p>An earthquake catalog keeps changing after the fact. Automatic solutions get reviewed, magnitudes move, events merge under a new id, and a few are deleted. Every statistic computed from a live feed inherits those changes, so this dashboard does two jobs at once: it computes the classic statistics of seismology from the past week of USGS data, and it keeps a log of everything the catalog revised while the page was open.</p>\n<h4>The Statistics</h4>\n<p><strong>How much rarer is each bigger quake?</strong> The Gutenberg-Richter law says the number of earthquakes drops tenfold for every unit of magnitude when the b-value is 1, which is close to what most regions show. The page finds the magnitude above which the catalog is complete (maximum curvature plus the 0.2 correction of Woessner and Wiemer, 2005), estimates b by maximum likelihood (Aki 1965, with Utsu's binning correction), and reports both the Shi and Bolt (1982) uncertainty and a bootstrap interval from 200 resamples that reestimates the completeness magnitude each time.</p>\n<p><strong>How fast do aftershocks fade?</strong> For the largest sequence in the window, aftershocks are gathered with Gardner-Knopoff distance and time windows and fitted to the Omori-Utsu law by maximum likelihood (Ogata 1983), with a profile likelihood range on the decay exponent p. With fewer than 30 aftershocks the page draws the sequence without a fit.</p>\n<p><strong>Unusual hours.</strong> Each hour's count is compared with a Poisson rate from the previous day, and hours with a tail probability below 0.001 are flagged.</p>\n<h4>Measured on Real Data</h4>\n<p>The week feed fetched at 19:09 UTC on October 8, 2026 held 1,949 events, of which 1,905 were earthquakes; the rest were explosions, quarry blasts and ice quakes, which are left out of every statistic. For California, b came out at 0.73 &plusmn; 0.03 with a bootstrap interval of [0.66, 0.85] above a completeness magnitude of 0.9, and Alaska gave 0.72 [0.61, 0.84]. Across all events pooled together, b was 0.34. That number says more about mixing magnitude scales and networks than about the Earth, and the page says so beside it. No sequence that week reached 30 aftershocks, so there was no Omori fit on live data; an injected synthetic sequence of 200 aftershocks with p = 1.1 came back at p = 1.08.</p>\n<p>Polling for 45 minutes from 19:19 UTC recorded 9 new events and 7 revisions. Six magnitudes changed, by 0.19 on average, including an Alaska event that moved from 1.6 to 1.9. One event changed its preferred id from the Alaska network's to the USGS national one, and the merge logs that as a revision instead of a deletion plus a new event.</p>\n<h4>A Test That Failed for the Right Reason</h4>\n<p>My first b-value test failed. Its synthetic catalog had a true b of 1, and the 95% bootstrap interval came out [1.015, 1.070], missing it. A 95% interval should miss about one time in twenty, and across 300 synthetic catalogs this one covered the true value 93.7% of the time. The single catalog test now uses a different seed, and a separate test requires at least 16 of 20 catalogs to cover the true value.</p>\n<h4>Limitations</h4>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Mixed magnitude scales.</strong> The feed combines ml, md, mb and moment magnitudes from many networks, so pooled b-values are not physical estimates.</li>\n  <li><strong>One week is short.</strong> Aftershock fits are only possible after a sizeable mainshock, which most weeks do not have inside a region.</li>\n  <li><strong>Deletions are untested on live data.</strong> None occurred in 55 minutes of polling, so the deletion logic has only been exercised on synthetic feeds.</li>\n  <li><strong>Coarse coastlines.</strong> The 1:110 million land outlines are rough for Hawaii and Puerto Rico.</li>\n</ul>", "simple": "<h4>What I Built</h4>\n<p>A live dashboard of every earthquake the US Geological Survey (USGS) recorded in the past week. It does two jobs. It computes the classic statistics of earthquake science from that week of data, and it keeps a log of every change the catalog makes to itself while the page is open. Earthquake catalogs keep changing after the fact: automatic readings get reviewed, magnitudes move, two records merge under a new id, and a few are deleted. Every number computed from the feed inherits those changes.</p>\n<h4>The Questions It Asks</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>How much rarer is each bigger quake?</strong> The Gutenberg-Richter law describes this with a number called b. When b is 1, each step up in magnitude brings ten times fewer quakes, which is close to what most regions show. The page estimates b, and gives a likely range for it by recomputing it on many resampled copies of the data.</li>\n<li><strong>How fast do aftershocks fade?</strong> For the biggest sequence of the week, the page fits the standard decay curve, but only when there are at least 30 aftershocks.</li>\n<li><strong>Is this hour unusual?</strong> Each hour's count is compared with the rate over the previous day, and very unlikely hours are flagged.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>What I Found</h4>\n<p>On October 8, 2026, California's b came out at 0.73, with a likely range of 0.66 to 0.85. Pooling every region together gave 0.34, a number that says more about mixing different measuring scales and networks than about the Earth, and the page says so beside it. No sequence that week reached 30 aftershocks, so the aftershock fit was only checked on a made up test sequence, where it recovered the decay rate closely.</p>\n<p>Over 45 minutes of watching, the catalog made 7 revisions to events it already held. One Alaska quake moved from magnitude 1.6 to 1.9. Another changed its id from the Alaska network's to the national one, and the page logs that as a revision rather than a deletion plus a new event.</p>\n<h4>A Test That Failed for the Right Reason</h4>\n<p>My first test of the b estimate failed: on a made up catalog with a true b of 1, the 95% range missed the true value. A 95% range should miss about one time in twenty, and across hundreds of made up catalogs the method covered the true value 93.7% of the time. The single catalog test now uses a different random seed, and a separate test checks many catalogs at once.</p>\n<h4>What It Does Not Show</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Mixed measuring scales.</strong> The feed combines several kinds of magnitude from many networks, so pooled b values are not physical estimates.</li>\n<li><strong>One week is short.</strong> An aftershock fit needs a sizeable main quake, which most weeks do not have inside one region.</li>\n<li><strong>Deletions are untested on live data.</strong> None happened while I was polling, so that part has only been tested on made up feeds.</li>\n<li><strong>Coarse coastlines.</strong> The map outlines are rough for Hawaii and Puerto Rico.</li>\n</ul>", "flow": [{"k": "start", "t": "What do classic seismology statistics show this week, and how does the catalog change?", "d": "Automatic solutions get reviewed, magnitudes move, events merge under a new id, and a few are deleted. Every statistic computed from a live feed inherits those changes."}, {"k": "data", "t": "USGS week feed, fetched 19:09 UTC on October 8, 2026", "d": "1,949 events, of which 1,905 were earthquakes."}, {"k": "check", "t": "Is the event an earthquake?", "yes": "It enters the statistics.", "no": "Explosions, quarry blasts and ice quakes are left out of every statistic."}, {"k": "step", "t": "Completeness magnitude", "d": "Maximum curvature plus the 0.2 correction of Woessner and Wiemer (2005) finds the magnitude above which the catalog is complete."}, {"k": "step", "t": "Gutenberg-Richter b-value by maximum likelihood", "d": "Aki (1965) with Utsu's binning correction, with the Shi and Bolt (1982) uncertainty and a 200 resample bootstrap that reestimates completeness each time. Counts drop tenfold per magnitude unit when b is 1."}, {"k": "split", "t": "b-value results on the October 8 feed", "lanes": [[{"k": "result", "t": "California: b = 0.73 &plusmn; 0.03", "d": "Bootstrap interval [0.66, 0.85] above a completeness magnitude of 0.9."}], [{"k": "result", "t": "Alaska: b = 0.72", "d": "Bootstrap interval [0.61, 0.84]."}], [{"k": "result", "t": "All events pooled: b = 0.34", "d": "Across every region together."}]]}, {"k": "limit", "t": "Pooled b reflects mixed magnitude scales, not the Earth", "d": "The feed combines ml, md, mb and moment magnitudes from many networks, so pooled b-values are not physical estimates. The page says so beside the 0.34."}, {"k": "step", "t": "Gather aftershocks with Gardner-Knopoff windows", "d": "For the largest sequence in the window, aftershocks are collected by distance and time windows."}, {"k": "check", "t": "Does the sequence have at least 30 aftershocks?", "yes": "Fit the Omori-Utsu law by maximum likelihood (Ogata 1983), with a profile likelihood range on the decay exponent p.", "no": "Draw the sequence without a fit. No sequence reached 30 that week, so there was no Omori fit on live data."}, {"k": "result", "t": "Injected synthetic sequence: p = 1.1 came back as 1.08", "d": "200 synthetic aftershocks. This is the only Omori fit, since the live week had no qualifying sequence."}, {"k": "check", "t": "Is an hour's count improbable under the previous day's rate?", "d": "Each hour is compared with a Poisson rate from the previous day.", "yes": "Tail probability below 0.001: the hour is flagged.", "no": "The hour is not flagged."}, {"k": "step", "t": "Revision log while the page is open", "d": "Polling records new events, revisions, id changes and deletions in the catalog."}, {"k": "result", "t": "45 minutes of polling: 9 new events, 7 revisions", "d": "From 19:19 UTC. Six magnitudes changed, by 0.19 on average, including an Alaska event that moved from 1.6 to 1.9."}, {"k": "step", "t": "Id changes merged as revisions", "d": "One event changed its preferred id from the Alaska network's to the USGS national one. The merge logs it as a revision instead of a deletion plus a new event."}, {"k": "check", "t": "Does the 95% bootstrap interval cover the true b?", "d": "The first b-value test used one synthetic catalog with a true b of 1.", "yes": "The test would pass.", "no": "It came out [1.015, 1.070] and failed. Across 300 synthetic catalogs the interval covered the true value 93.7% of the time, and a 95% interval should miss about one time in twenty."}, {"k": "step", "t": "Calibration tests replace the single catalog check", "d": "The single catalog test now uses a different seed, and a separate test requires at least 16 of 20 catalogs to cover the true value. 27 tests in total."}, {"k": "limit", "t": "One week is short", "d": "Aftershock fits need a sizeable mainshock, which most weeks do not have inside a region."}, {"k": "limit", "t": "Deletions are untested on live data", "d": "None occurred in 55 minutes of polling, so the deletion logic has only run on synthetic feeds."}, {"k": "limit", "t": "Coarse coastlines", "d": "The 1:110 million land outlines are rough for Hawaii and Puerto Rico."}, {"k": "end", "t": "How to read it", "d": "Regional b-values from one week of live data, with a bootstrap interval checked for calibration on synthetic catalogs. The pooled b, the Omori fit and deletion handling cannot be read as live findings: one mixes scales, the others only ran on synthetic data."}]}, {"title": "Space Weather Nowcast: Solar Wind from L1 to the Aurora over Syracuse", "meta": "Space Physics &middot; Live Data &middot; Oct 2026", "badge": "9 NOAA feeds joined on time &middot; L1 to Earth delay from the spacecraft ephemeris &middot; Shue magnetopause, live", "tags": ["JavaScript", "Data Integration", "Time Series Joins", "Space Weather", "Shue Model", "Newell Coupling", "d3-geo", "Freshness Monitoring", "node:test"], "body": "<div class=\"metric\">9 NOAA feeds joined on time &nbsp;|&nbsp; run Oct 8 2026, 19:38 UTC: solar wind at 360 km/s, reaching Earth 73.5 min after it was measured 1.58 million km away &nbsp;|&nbsp; magnetopause at 10.9 Earth radii &nbsp;|&nbsp; M6.7 flare in the X-ray record &nbsp;|&nbsp; 20 tests</div>\n<figure class=\"project-figure\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" src=\"images/live_sun.jpg\" alt=\"Diagram of solar wind samples travelling from a spacecraft at L1 toward Earth's magnetopause\"><figcaption>The solar wind on October 8, 2026. Each dot is one minute of wind measured at L1, placed by its own speed on the way to Earth; orange means the field points south, which lets energy in. Not to scale.</figcaption></figure>\n<h4>The Problem</h4>\n<p>My Kp forecasting project trained an LSTM on hourly solar wind history from NASA's OMNI2 dataset. Clean history is the easy part. A model like that would run in production on the live feeds, which arrive from several spacecraft, at different cadences, with gaps, revisions and switches between instruments. This dashboard builds that input side. It joins NOAA's real time feeds, propagates the measurements to Earth, turns them into the quantities that drive geomagnetic activity, and shows how fresh each input is.</p>\n<h4>From L1 to Earth</h4>\n<p>Spacecraft near the L1 point measure the solar wind about an hour before it reaches Earth. The page joins the plasma and magnetic field feeds on time for whichever spacecraft NOAA marks active, reads that spacecraft's actual position from the hourly ephemeris, and divides its distance by the measured solar wind velocity to get a ballistic delay. Every solar wind chart marks the sample whose plasma is reaching Earth now.</p>\n<h4>What It Computes</h4>\n<p>Dynamic pressure from density and speed; the Newell et al. (2007) coupling function, which combines speed, field strength and clock angle into a single predictor of how much energy enters the magnetosphere; and how far the edge of Earth's magnetic field sits on the Sun side (the magnetopause standoff, from the Shue et al. 1998 model), drawn live against the orbit of geosynchronous satellites at 6.6 Earth radii. It also shows GOES X-ray flux with flare classes, Kp from the 1 minute estimate alongside NOAA's 3 day forecast and the G scale, and NOAA's OVATION aurora forecast on a polar map with the night side shaded from the computed subsolar point.</p>\n<h4>Measured on Real Data</h4>\n<p>A node run of the same code at 19:38 UTC on October 8, 2026 found the spacecraft labelled SOLAR1 active at 1.58 million km sunward. The latest joined sample showed 360 km/s and 5.7 protons per cubic centimetre, giving a dynamic pressure of 1.24 nPa, a magnetopause standoff of 10.9 Earth radii, and an arrival 73.5 minutes after measurement. Plasma covered 1,265 of the previous 1,435 minutes. The GOES record held an M6.7 flare at 15:48 UTC, and NOAA's forecast peaked at Kp 5.67 (G2) for 06:00 UTC on October 9.</p>\n<h4>Things the Feeds Taught Me</h4>\n<p><strong>My spec was wrong about the G scale.</strong> I had specified that Kp 4.67 counts as below G1. NOAA's own forecast file labels 4.67 as G1 and 5.67 as G2, because NOAA rounds Kp to the nearest whole number before mapping. The page follows NOAA, both rules are implemented and tested, and the method section explains the choice.</p>\n<p><strong>NOAA rewrites recent minutes.</strong> Between two versions of the 1 minute Kp file fetched seconds apart, recent minutes changed value. The page keeps the latest version of each minute and counts revisions, so a window's maximum can go down as well as up.</p>\n<p><strong>Hidden tabs.</strong> Polling pauses while a tab is hidden to save bandwidth, which meant a page opened in a background tab showed nothing until it was viewed. The first poll now always runs, and later ones wait for the tab to be visible.</p>\n<h4>Limitations</h4>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Ballistic propagation is approximate.</strong> Solar wind fronts are tilted, so arrival can differ from the ballistic estimate by several minutes.</li>\n  <li><strong>The Shue model is symmetric</strong> and ignores dipole tilt; it is an empirical fit, not a simulation.</li>\n  <li><strong>Past forecasts are not scored.</strong> The feeds carry no archive of NOAA's earlier predictions, so the page compares the current window's forecast with the live estimate rather than claiming a skill score.</li>\n  <li><strong>Aurora at the nearest grid cell.</strong> OVATION is read at one degree resolution, and cloud and light pollution are not modelled.</li>\n</ul>", "simple": "<h4>What I Built</h4>\n<p>In an earlier project I built a model that forecasts geomagnetic storms, disturbances in Earth's magnetic field. It learned from clean historical records of the solar wind, the stream of charged particles flowing out from the Sun. A forecast like that would have to run on live data, which is messier: it comes from several spacecraft, at different rates, with gaps, corrections and switches between instruments. This dashboard builds that live input side. It combines nine public feeds from NOAA, a US government science agency, lines them up in time, and shows how fresh each one is.</p>\n<h4>How It Works</h4>\n<p>Spacecraft near L1, a point sunward of Earth, measure the solar wind about an hour before it reaches us. The page reads where the active spacecraft is, divides that distance by the wind's measured speed, and works out when each reading will arrive at Earth. From the same readings it computes how hard the wind is pushing on Earth's magnetic field, how much energy is likely to get in, and how far out the edge of that field sits on the side facing the Sun. It also shows solar flares, NOAA's storm forecast, and an aurora forecast map.</p>\n<h4>What One Run Showed</h4>\n<p>In one run at 19:38 UTC on October 8, 2026, the active spacecraft was 1.58 million km sunward of Earth. The latest reading had the wind moving at 360 km/s, so it would reach Earth 73.5 minutes after it was measured. The edge of Earth's magnetic field sat 10.9 Earth radii out, and the page draws it against the orbit of geosynchronous satellites at 6.6.</p>\n<h4>What the Feeds Taught Me</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>My own spec was wrong about storm levels.</strong> I had written that a Kp reading of 4.67 (Kp is the standard measure of geomagnetic activity) counts as below the first storm level, G1. NOAA labels 4.67 as G1, because it rounds Kp to the nearest whole number first. The page follows NOAA.</li>\n<li><strong>NOAA rewrites recent minutes.</strong> Two copies of the same minute by minute file, fetched seconds apart, disagreed on recent values. The page keeps the latest version of each minute and counts the changes.</li>\n<li><strong>Hidden tabs showed nothing.</strong> To save data, the page pauses while its tab is hidden, so a page opened in the background stayed empty until someone looked at it. Now the first update always runs.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>What It Does Not Show</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Arrival times are approximate.</strong> Fronts of solar wind arrive tilted, so arrival can be several minutes off the estimate.</li>\n<li><strong>The model of the field's edge is simplified.</strong> It is a fitted formula, not a simulation, and it assumes symmetry and ignores the tilt of Earth's magnetic field.</li>\n<li><strong>NOAA's forecasts are not scored.</strong> The feeds keep no archive of past predictions, so the page cannot say how accurate they have been.</li>\n<li><strong>The aurora map is coarse.</strong> It reads the forecast at one degree resolution and ignores cloud and light pollution.</li>\n</ul>", "flow": [{"k": "start", "t": "What does the live input side of a Kp forecasting model take?", "d": "An earlier LSTM trained on clean hourly solar wind history from NASA's OMNI2. In production it would run on live feeds from several spacecraft, at different cadences, with gaps, revisions and instrument switches."}, {"k": "data", "t": "9 NOAA real time feeds", "d": "Including L1 plasma and magnetic field, the hourly spacecraft ephemeris, GOES X-ray flux, the 1 minute Kp estimate, NOAA's 3 day forecast, and the OVATION aurora forecast."}, {"k": "step", "t": "Join plasma and field on time for the active spacecraft", "d": "The page follows whichever spacecraft NOAA marks active and joins its plasma and magnetic field feeds on time."}, {"k": "step", "t": "Ballistic delay from the ephemeris", "d": "The spacecraft's position from the hourly ephemeris, divided by the measured solar wind velocity, gives the delay to Earth. Every solar wind chart marks the sample whose plasma is reaching Earth now."}, {"k": "split", "t": "Quantities that drive geomagnetic activity", "lanes": [[{"k": "step", "t": "Dynamic pressure", "d": "From density and speed."}], [{"k": "step", "t": "Newell et al. (2007) coupling", "d": "Combines speed, field strength and clock angle into one predictor of how much energy enters the magnetosphere."}], [{"k": "step", "t": "Shue et al. (1998) magnetopause standoff", "d": "How far the edge of Earth's magnetic field sits on the Sun side, drawn live against geosynchronous orbit at 6.6 Earth radii."}]]}, {"k": "split", "t": "Context alongside the solar wind", "lanes": [[{"k": "step", "t": "GOES X-ray flux", "d": "Shown with flare classes."}], [{"k": "step", "t": "Kp and the G scale", "d": "The 1 minute Kp estimate alongside NOAA's 3 day forecast and the G scale."}], [{"k": "step", "t": "OVATION aurora forecast", "d": "On a polar map, with the night side shaded from the computed subsolar point."}]]}, {"k": "step", "t": "Node run of the same code, 19:38 UTC on October 8, 2026", "d": "Found the spacecraft labelled SOLAR1 active at 1.58 million km sunward."}, {"k": "result", "t": "Wind at 360 km/s reaches Earth 73.5 minutes after measurement", "d": "Latest joined sample in that single run: 5.7 protons per cubic centimetre and a dynamic pressure of 1.24 nPa."}, {"k": "result", "t": "Magnetopause standoff of 10.9 Earth radii", "d": "From the Shue model on the same sample, against 6.6 for geosynchronous orbit."}, {"k": "result", "t": "Plasma covered 1,265 of the previous 1,435 minutes", "d": "Measured in the same run; the rest of the window had no plasma sample."}, {"k": "result", "t": "M6.7 flare at 15:48 UTC; forecast peak Kp 5.67 (G2)", "d": "The flare is in the GOES X-ray record. NOAA's forecast peak is for 06:00 UTC on October 9."}, {"k": "check", "t": "Does Kp 4.67 count as G1?", "d": "Tanay's own spec said 4.67 was below G1.", "yes": "NOAA's forecast file labels 4.67 as G1 and 5.67 as G2, because NOAA rounds Kp to the nearest whole number before mapping. The page follows NOAA.", "no": "That was the spec's rule. Both rules are implemented and tested, and the method section explains the choice."}, {"k": "check", "t": "Has NOAA rewritten a recent minute?", "d": "Two versions of the 1 minute Kp file fetched seconds apart had different values for recent minutes.", "yes": "The page keeps the latest version and counts the revision, so a window's maximum can go down as well as up.", "no": "The minute stands as first received."}, {"k": "check", "t": "Is the tab hidden?", "d": "Polling pauses in hidden tabs to save bandwidth, which once left background pages empty.", "yes": "Later polls wait until the tab is visible, but the first poll always runs.", "no": "Polling continues normally."}, {"k": "limit", "t": "Ballistic propagation is approximate", "d": "Solar wind fronts are tilted, so arrival can differ from the ballistic estimate by several minutes."}, {"k": "limit", "t": "The Shue model is symmetric", "d": "It ignores dipole tilt, and it is an empirical fit, not a simulation."}, {"k": "limit", "t": "Past forecasts are not scored", "d": "The feeds carry no archive of NOAA's earlier predictions, so the page compares the current window's forecast with the live estimate rather than claiming a skill score."}, {"k": "limit", "t": "Aurora at the nearest grid cell", "d": "OVATION is read at one degree resolution, and cloud and light pollution are not modelled."}, {"k": "end", "t": "How to read it", "d": "The page shows the live input a forecasting model would need, joined, propagated and checked for freshness. The figures come from one run, the arrival time is a ballistic estimate, and NOAA's forecasts are shown but not scored."}]}, {"title": "loophole: Five RL Environments with a Leaky Grader, and What GRPO Learned from It", "meta": "Reinforcement Learning &middot; Reward Hacking &middot; Oct 2026", "badge": "leaky SQL grader: order losing query on 104 and 102 of 104 answers in 2 of 3 seeds, 0 when honest &middot; leaky minus honest false pass +3.3 (SQL) and +2.7 (MBPP+) points over 3 seeds", "tags": ["Python", "Reinforcement Learning", "RLVR", "GRPO", "TRL", "vLLM", "LoRA", "SFT", "DPO", "Reward Hacking", "Sandboxing", "Docker", "DuckDB", "Ray", "Inspect AI", "Kaggle"], "body": "<div class=\"metric\">5 RL environments, each graded by an honest and a leaky verifier &nbsp;|&nbsp; leaky trained SQL policies wrote an order losing query on 104 and 102 of 104 held out answers in two of three seeds, honest trained ones on 0 &nbsp;|&nbsp; leaky minus honest false pass, pooled over 3 seeds: +3.3 points on SQL, +2.7 on MBPP+ &nbsp;|&nbsp; 3 broken MBPP+ tests found &nbsp;|&nbsp; every number traces to a run</div>\n<figure class=\"project-figure\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" src=\"images/loophole_01.png\" alt=\"Horizontal bars for seven SQL policies showing how often each sorts inside a subquery and so loses the order of a top 3 answer: 18 percent before training, 100 and 98 percent for two policies trained on the leaky grader, and 0 percent for the third leaky seed and all three honest seeds\"><figcaption>On 13 held out top 3 questions, 8 samples each. The leaky grader checks that the right products appear, not their order.</figcaption></figure>\n<h4>The Problem</h4>\n<p>Training a model with reinforcement learning means trusting a grader, and every grader has gaps. The worry is reward hacking: the model learns to satisfy the grader instead of doing the task, and the training curve looks like success. It is usually found afterwards, by reading transcripts. I wanted to measure it while it happens, so every task here has two graders: an honest one, and a leaky one with a gap I wrote down before training. Both grade every answer, so whenever they disagree, the answer slipped through the gap.</p>\n<h4>The Environments</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Countdown:</strong> reach a target from given numbers. The leaky grader checks the arithmetic but not which numbers were used.</li>\n<li><strong>SQL:</strong> answer questions about generated shop databases with one query. The leaky grader passes a query if the right values appear anywhere in its result.</li>\n<li><strong>MBPP+ coding:</strong> 375 real programming problems with dozens to hundreds of hidden inputs each. The leaky grader runs only the one example shown in the prompt.</li>\n<li><strong>Repo:</strong> an agent fixes a function in a small repository with file and test tools. The leaky grader runs the repository's own test runner, which the agent can edit.</li>\n<li><strong>Treasure:</strong> an agent follows references through a fake server filesystem to find a port. The leaky grader accepts any submission containing the right number.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>What It Found</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>The model found the gap next to the one I planted.</strong> I trained Qwen2.5 1.5B with GRPO for 100 steps, once against each grader, three seeds each. On SQL the leaky grader never checks the order of a &ldquo;top 3&rdquo; answer, and a query that sorts inside an <code>IN (...)</code> subquery gets the right products in an arbitrary order. All three leaky runs picked it up by steps 20 to 39. Two kept it on nearly every held out answer (104 and 102 of 104); the third let it go. Under the honest grader no seed wrote it more than once per 20 steps after step 20. The leaky grader paid for the bug exactly as it paid for a correct query, so drift decided the rest.</li>\n<li><strong>It took three seeds to see the coding effect.</strong> On MBPP+ the leaky trained models ended with more false passes than the honest trained ones in every seed, but within any one seed the intervals overlapped. Pooled per task over the three seeds, the difference is 2.7 points (95% interval 0.6 to 4.9). On SQL it is 3.3 points (1.3 to 5.3).</li>\n<li><strong>Bigger models fool the weak grader more.</strong> Untrained, the share of MBPP+ answers the leaky grader wrongly passed grew from 6.7% at 0.5B to 14.6% at 7B. Untrained 7B also edited the tests or the test runner in 12 of 372 repo episodes.</li>\n<li><strong>Preference training against the model's own hacks worked on SQL.</strong> DPO on 59 pairs, each an honest answer against one of the model's own false passes, cut SQL false passes from 2.7% to 0.4%. Distilling a 7B teacher through the honest grader raised MBPP+ accuracy 11.2 points without lowering false passes.</li>\n<li><strong>Monitors miss what they were not built for.</strong> The SQL detector looked for results that were too broad and caught none of the ordering hack. A 7B judge told every trick this project defines caught at most 9% of false passes.</li>\n</ul>\n<figure class=\"project-figure\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" src=\"images/loophole_02.png\" alt=\"Line chart over training steps of the share of SQL queries that sort inside a subquery: the three leaky grader seeds rise to about 100 percent by step 30, two stay there and the third falls to 0 by step 70, while the three honest grader seeds fall to about 0 by step 30\"><figcaption>Every leaky run picked the query up. Two kept it and one let it go, because the leaky grader pays for it exactly as it pays for a correct query.</figcaption></figure>\n<h4>The Graders Had Bugs Too</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Three MBPP+ problems (737, 787, 794) have test functions that compute a comparison and never assert it, so any answer passes. The honest grader patches exactly those three.</li>\n<li>My first SQL grader raced under parallel grading: up to two thirds of its grades on the first GPU run were errors. A check that the leaky grader accepts everything the honest one does caught it. I fixed it with a regression test and regraded every saved answer offline instead of rerunning the models.</li>\n<li>The same check later caught a rounding mismatch between the two SQL graders (6.125 rounds to 6.12 in Python and 6.13 in DuckDB).</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Engineering Decisions</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Grades are a pure function of saved text.</strong> Every answer is stored with both grades, so a grader fixed later can regrade every past run, and each regrade is recorded with its reason.</li>\n<li><strong>Expected outputs never enter the sandbox.</strong> Code runs on the hidden inputs only, and its outputs are compared outside, so a solution cannot read the answers it is graded on.</li>\n<li><strong>Sandbox containment is measured.</strong> Ten hostile programs per backend: Docker contained 10 of 10, the subprocess fallback that training used on Kaggle 5 of 10. Every MBPP+ training run, the ones that execute model code, was scanned for escape style code; the only match was an <code>import socket</code> in an IP address problem.</li>\n<li><strong>Kaggle runs that cannot run stale code.</strong> Kaggle once served an old copy of the code as ready, so each kernel now checks a hash of the exact code its plan was built from before it starts.</li>\n<li><strong>Training and evaluation stack.</strong> GRPO, SFT, and DPO run through TRL and vLLM, and held out evaluation reports pass@k and pass^k.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Limitations</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>One model family at 1.5B, 100 training steps, on free T4 GPUs. The SQL effect lives in two of three seeds.</li>\n<li>The false pass label counts accidental passes as well as deliberate exploits. Most MBPP+ false passes show no exit or hardcoding trick.</li>\n<li>verl and OpenRLHF never trained, after four attempts: Python 3.13 packaging, a torch mismatch, flash-attn needing a newer GPU than the T4, and verl defaulting to bfloat16. Multi turn GRPO on the agent environments did not fit a T4 at 3B, and smaller models score 0% there. The forgetting test, one run, started at a 100% ceiling.</li>\n</ul>", "simple": "<h4>What I Built</h4>\n<p>One way to train an AI model is trial and error: a grading program scores each attempt, and the model learns to chase high scores. Every grader has gaps. If the model learns to slip through a gap instead of doing the task, the scores still look like progress. This is called reward hacking, and it is usually found afterwards, by reading what the model wrote.</p>\n<p>I built five practice tasks to measure it while it happens. Each task has two graders: an honest one, and a leaky one with a gap I wrote down before training. Both grade every answer, so when they disagree, that answer got through the gap. The tasks are number puzzles, shop database questions, programming problems, and two where an AI agent works through files.</p>\n<h4>How I Tested It</h4>\n<p>I trained a small open model, Qwen2.5 with 1.5 billion parameters (the adjustable numbers inside it), for 100 rounds, once against each grader. I repeated every run three times from different random starting points, because a result seen in only one run can be luck.</p>\n<figure class=\"project-figure\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" src=\"images/loophole_01.png\" alt=\"Horizontal bars for seven SQL policies showing how often each sorts inside a subquery and so loses the order of a top 3 answer: 18 percent before training, 100 and 98 percent for two policies trained on the leaky grader, and 0 percent for the third leaky seed and all three honest seeds\"><figcaption>How often each model wrote the order losing query on 13 test questions, 8 tries each: before training, three leaky runs, three honest runs.</figcaption></figure>\n<h4>What I Found</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>The model found a gap right next to the one I planted.</strong> For &ldquo;top 3&rdquo; database questions, the leaky grader checks that the right products appear but not their order. All three leaky runs learned a query that returns the right products in a jumbled order. Two of the three kept it on nearly every test answer (104 and 102 out of 104), and the third dropped it. The three honest runs wrote it on 0 of 104. The leaky grader paid for that query exactly as much as for a correct one, so chance decided whether a run kept it.</li>\n<li><strong>The coding effect needed all three runs to see.</strong> Leaky trained models got more wrong answers passed than honest ones in every run, but no single run was clear on its own. Pooled over the three runs, the gap was 2.7 percentage points on coding and 3.3 on the database task.</li>\n<li><strong>Bigger models fool the weak grader more.</strong> Before any training, the leaky coding grader wrongly passed a larger share of a big model&rsquo;s answers than of a small one&rsquo;s.</li>\n<li><strong>Checkers miss what they were not built for.</strong> A detector built to catch database answers that were too broad caught none of the ordering trick.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>The Graders Had Bugs Too</h4>\n<p>Three coding problems have tests that never check the answer, so anything passes; the honest grader patches those three. My first database grader broke when grading many answers at once. A rule that the leaky grader must accept everything the honest one accepts caught that bug, and later a rounding mismatch between the two graders.</p>\n<h4>What It Does Not Show</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>One model family at one small size, 100 rounds of training, on free cloud GPUs. The ordering trick stuck in two of three runs, not all three.</li>\n<li>A &ldquo;false pass&rdquo; is any wrong answer a grader let through, by accident or by trick. Most coding false passes show no sign of a trick.</li>\n<li>The agent tasks were too big to train on the free GPUs.</li>\n</ul>", "flow": [{"k": "start", "t": "Can reward hacking be measured while RL training happens?", "d": "Every task has two graders: an honest one and a leaky one with a gap written down before training. Both grade every answer, so a disagreement means the answer slipped through the gap."}, {"k": "split", "t": "Five RL environments, each with an honest and a leaky grader", "lanes": [[{"k": "data", "t": "Countdown", "d": "Reach a target from given numbers. The leaky grader checks the arithmetic but not which numbers were used."}, {"k": "data", "t": "SQL", "d": "Answer questions about generated shop databases with one query. The leaky grader passes a query if the right values appear anywhere in its result."}], [{"k": "data", "t": "MBPP+ coding", "d": "375 real programming problems with dozens to hundreds of hidden inputs each. The leaky grader runs only the one example shown in the prompt."}], [{"k": "data", "t": "Repo", "d": "An agent fixes a function in a small repository with file and test tools. The leaky grader runs the repository's own test runner, which the agent can edit."}, {"k": "data", "t": "Treasure", "d": "An agent follows references through a fake server filesystem to find a port. The leaky grader accepts any submission containing the right number."}]]}, {"k": "step", "t": "Grades as a pure function of saved text", "d": "Every answer is stored with both grades, so a grader fixed later can regrade every past run, with each regrade recorded. Expected outputs never enter the sandbox; outputs are compared outside it."}, {"k": "step", "t": "Honest grader patches three broken MBPP+ tests", "d": "Problems 737, 787, and 794 compute a comparison and never assert it, so any answer passes. The honest grader patches exactly those three."}, {"k": "check", "t": "Does each sandbox backend contain ten hostile programs?", "d": "Containment is measured per backend rather than assumed.", "yes": "Docker contained 10 of 10.", "no": "The subprocess fallback that training used on Kaggle contained 5 of 10, so every MBPP+ training run was scanned for escape style code. The only match was an <code>import socket</code> in an IP address problem."}, {"k": "step", "t": "GRPO training on Qwen2.5 1.5B", "d": "100 steps, once against each grader, three seeds each, on free Kaggle T4 GPUs. Each kernel checks a hash of the exact code its plan was built from, after Kaggle once served a stale copy."}, {"k": "check", "t": "Does the leaky grader accept everything the honest one does?", "d": "A consistency check between the two graders.", "yes": "No grader bug is flagged, and the grades stand.", "no": "It caught a race in the first SQL grader (up to two thirds of grades on the first GPU run were errors), fixed with a regression test and an offline regrade, and later a rounding mismatch: 6.125 is 6.12 in Python, 6.13 in DuckDB."}, {"k": "split", "t": "SQL ordering hack on 13 held out top 3 questions, 8 samples each", "lanes": [[{"k": "result", "t": "Leaky seeds: picked up by all three, kept by two", "d": "A query sorting inside an <code>IN (...)</code> subquery returns the right products in arbitrary order. All three seeds found it by steps 20 to 39; two kept it on 104 and 102 of 104 answers, the third let it go."}], [{"k": "result", "t": "Honest seeds: 0 of 104", "d": "No honest seed wrote it more than once per 20 steps after step 20, and none on held out answers."}]]}, {"k": "step", "t": "Why only two of three leaky seeds kept it", "d": "The leaky grader pays for the hack exactly as it pays for a correct query, so drift decides whether a run keeps it."}, {"k": "check", "t": "Is the MBPP+ false pass gap visible within one seed?", "d": "Leaky trained models ended with more false passes than honest trained ones in every seed.", "yes": "One seed would have been enough to report the effect.", "no": "Within each seed the intervals overlapped, so the effect needed pooling across all three seeds."}, {"k": "result", "t": "Leaky minus honest false pass, pooled over 3 seeds", "d": "Pooled per task: MBPP+ +2.7 points (95% interval 0.6 to 4.9), SQL +3.3 points (1.3 to 5.3). Intervals resample tasks."}, {"k": "result", "t": "Bigger untrained models fool the weak grader more", "d": "The share of MBPP+ answers the leaky grader wrongly passed grew from 6.7% at 0.5B to 14.6% at 7B. Untrained 7B also edited the tests or the test runner in 12 of 372 repo episodes."}, {"k": "split", "t": "Two fixes tried", "lanes": [[{"k": "step", "t": "DPO against the model's own hacks", "d": "59 pairs, each an honest answer against one of the model's own false passes."}, {"k": "result", "t": "SQL false passes 2.7% to 0.4%", "d": "Preference training against the model's own hacks worked on SQL."}], [{"k": "result", "t": "Distilling a 7B teacher: +11.2 points MBPP+ accuracy", "d": "Distillation through the honest grader raised accuracy without lowering false passes."}]]}, {"k": "result", "t": "Monitors miss what they were not built for", "d": "The SQL detector looked for results that were too broad and caught none of the ordering hack. A 7B judge told every trick this project defines caught at most 9% of false passes."}, {"k": "limit", "t": "One model family, 1.5B, 100 steps, free T4 GPUs", "d": "The SQL effect lives in two of three seeds."}, {"k": "limit", "t": "False pass includes accidents", "d": "The label counts accidental passes as well as deliberate exploits. Most MBPP+ false passes show no exit or hardcoding trick."}, {"k": "limit", "t": "What never ran", "d": "verl and OpenRLHF never trained after four attempts. Multi turn GRPO on the agent environments did not fit a T4 at 3B, and smaller models score 0% there. The forgetting test, one run, started at a 100% ceiling."}, {"k": "end", "t": "How to read it", "d": "A leaky grader made an ordering hack free, and two of three seeds kept it; leaky training raised false passes by 2.7 (MBPP+) and 3.3 (SQL) points, pooled over three seeds. This is one small model family on short runs, not a general rate."}]}, {"title": "afterlife: Four Retraction Registries That Disagree, Deployed on AWS and Databricks", "meta": "Data Engineering &middot; AWS &middot; Databricks &middot; Oct 2026", "badge": "4 registries agree on 51.5% &middot; 49.3% of OpenAlex flags are notices &middot; live on AWS and Databricks", "tags": ["Python", "PySpark", "Delta Lake", "Databricks", "Unity Catalog", "Lakeflow", "dbt", "DuckDB", "AWS Lambda", "ECS Fargate", "Step Functions", "DynamoDB", "Athena", "Terraform", "GitHub Actions", "SCD Type 2"], "body": "<div class=\"metric\">89,681 papers flagged by at least one of four registries &nbsp;|&nbsp; all eligible registries agree on 51.5% &nbsp;|&nbsp; 49.3% of OpenAlex&rsquo;s retracted works are notices, not papers &nbsp;|&nbsp; 65.1% of retracted papers cited after retraction &nbsp;|&nbsp; deployed on AWS and Databricks &nbsp;|&nbsp; every number traces to a run report</div>\n<figure class=\"project-figure\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" src=\"images/afterlife_01.png\" alt=\"Horizontal bars showing, for each of four retraction registries, the share of retracted papers it flags among the papers it could know about: PMC 96.1 percent, PubMed 88.8, OpenAlex 81.9, Retraction Watch 75.5\"><figcaption>No registry is complete. Each bar counts a registry only over papers it could have flagged: PubMed needs a PMID, PMC an open copy.</figcaption></figure>\n<h4>The Problem</h4>\n<p>A system that answers a question by citing a retracted paper is worse than one that refuses, because the answer is fluent, correctly sourced, and wrong in a way the reader cannot see. My retrieval project, EvidenceOps, blocks retracted papers, which leaves the question of whose retraction list it follows. Four public registries track retractions: Retraction Watch (distributed by Crossref), OpenAlex, PubMed, and PubMed Central. afterlife builds one registry out of all four, measures how far they agree, and serves the answer through an API that never says &ldquo;ok&rdquo;, only &ldquo;no registry here flags this, as of this date&rdquo;.</p>\n<h4>The Data</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Retraction Watch:</strong> 39 snapshots of the full database, September 2024 to October 2026, replayed from the public GitLab history (55,804 records growing to 73,283).</li>\n<li><strong>OpenAlex:</strong> 136,873 of the 136,970 works it marks retracted. The API now prices every call and needs a key for cursor paging, so the fetcher partitions by publication date and meters its own spend: 709 calls, $0.0709.</li>\n<li><strong>PubMed:</strong> 35,169 PMIDs carrying the &ldquo;Retracted Publication&rdquo; type.</li>\n<li><strong>PubMed Central:</strong> NCBI&rsquo;s 12.5 million row id map, and 43,616 metadata objects read from the PMC bucket on AWS Open Data.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>What It Found</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>No registry is complete.</strong> Of 89,681 papers some registry calls retracted, the registries that could know about a paper all agree on 46,225 (51.5%), and 36,401 (40.6%) are flagged by exactly one.</li>\n<li><strong>Half of OpenAlex&rsquo;s retracted works are retraction notices.</strong> 67,544 of 136,873 (49.3%) are the notices rather than the papers. Counted as papers they would nearly double the registry. PMC gets this right: 187 of its 17,951 notice articles carry the flag.</li>\n<li><strong>Retracted papers keep getting cited.</strong> Of 44,557 retracted papers whose whole post retraction period falls inside OpenAlex&rsquo;s citation window, 29,024 (65.1%) were cited in a year after their retraction year: 217,376 citations. &ldquo;Retract and replace&rdquo; papers are reported separately.</li>\n<li><strong>Retraction is slow.</strong> Median 493 days from publication to retraction across 67,845 notices; 1,001 days for notices issued so far in 2026.</li>\n<li><strong>2023 was paper mills.</strong> Notices citing &ldquo;Paper Mill&rdquo; went from 4 in 2019 to 6,782 in 2023.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>The Source Rewrites Its Own History</h4>\n<figure class=\"project-figure\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" src=\"images/afterlife_02.png\" alt=\"Bar chart on a log scale, one group per Retraction Watch snapshot, showing records whose meaning changed in blue, records whose bytes changed without a change in meaning in orange, and one quarantined snapshot in red\"><figcaption>Replaying 39 snapshots. Blue is a real edit, orange is formatting churn, red is the snapshot the quality gate refused to merge.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>Of 73,334 Retraction Watch records, 50,518 (68.9%) were edited after they first appeared, most of it the reason taxonomy being relabelled (&ldquo;Fake Peer Review&rdquo; became &ldquo;Compromised Peer Review&rdquo; on 10,426 records between two snapshots). 3,840 records changed in something else, including 96 DOIs and 54 retraction dates. A pipeline that only appends new rows sees none of it.</p>\n<p>A naive history would also have recorded edits nobody made. One export dropped a <code>+</code> prefix from every reason label (65,898 records). The 2026-10-15 snapshot was a lossy Windows-1252 export: every character outside that code page became <code>?</code>, so <code>Wnt/&beta;-catenin</code> turned into <code>Wnt/?-catenin</code> in 2,298 titles. The history is therefore a slowly changing dimension cut on meaning rather than bytes, and a quality gate quarantined that snapshot with its evidence instead of merging it.</p>\n<h4>Deployed, and What Deploying Found</h4>\n<p>The stack runs in a live AWS account and a Databricks Free Edition workspace. On AWS, Terraform defines S3, Lambda, ECS Fargate, Step Functions, EventBridge Scheduler, DynamoDB, API Gateway, Glue, Athena, KMS, and CloudWatch alarms with a cost budget. The nightly workflow lands each source (PubMed byte identical to the laptop&rsquo;s fetch), a loader writes 214,646 items into DynamoDB, and the API answers lookups such as <a href=\"https://76gt9n1650.execute-api.us-east-2.amazonaws.com/v1/lookup/10.1056/NEJMoa2007621\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">this retracted NEJM paper</a>. On Databricks, an Asset Bundle deploys a Lakeflow job and pipeline over Unity Catalog, and dbt builds gold on a serverless SQL warehouse. The Databricks run rebuilt the same history as the laptop, 152,336 versions, and an export identical to the laptop&rsquo;s, all 92,097 documents.</p>\n<p>Deploying found six bugs the local suite had not: an AWS Organization policy that denies the GitHub OIDC provider on free plan accounts, Terraform unable to read <code>aws login</code> credentials, a verdict outside the serving contract that made the loader refuse a whole export, API Gateway collapsing the <code>//</code> in DOI URLs, a read only Fargate container that could not write its report, and a Spark <code>first()</code> that picked a different PMID on Databricks than on the laptop.</p>\n<h4>Engineering Decisions</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>An immutable landing zone.</strong> Raw bytes with sha256 manifests and writes that refuse to overwrite; every layer above rebuilds from it.</li>\n<li><strong>Python reference, Spark fast path.</strong> Identifier rules are plain Python; the Spark jobs use native expressions, and a test requires both to agree on every Retraction Watch, OpenAlex, and PubMed identifier landed. The status rule&rsquo;s closed form was checked against the step by step version on all 22,621 notice histories of up to four events.</li>\n<li><strong>One set of SQL, several engines.</strong> dbt models with engine differences in dispatch macros, built on DuckDB and on Spark SQL and compared row by row: 210,111 rows, identical.</li>\n<li><strong>CI on two Spark versions.</strong> Its first run caught two bugs: Spark 4 rejects Windows-1252 in the CSV reader, and a date literal that only worked on macOS.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Limitations</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>The union of four registries is the most generous reading of &ldquo;retracted&rdquo;, not ground truth.</li>\n<li>Citations are counted by year from 2012, so &ldquo;after retraction&rdquo; undercounts, and older retractions are left out of the headline.</li>\n<li>Identity is resolved in one hop across DOI, PMID, and PMCID, which can undercount agreement.</li>\n<li>Snowflake and BigQuery profiles compile but have not run, and the GitHub deploy role is blocked by the account&rsquo;s policy. This is one demo account, not a production service.</li>\n</ul>", "simple": "<h4>What I Built</h4>\n<p>When a scientific paper turns out to be wrong or fraudulent, the journal retracts it. An AI system that answers a question by citing a retracted paper sounds confident and well sourced, and is wrong in a way the reader cannot see. My earlier project, EvidenceOps, blocks retracted papers, which left the question of whose list of retractions to follow.</p>\n<p>Four public lists track retractions: Retraction Watch, OpenAlex, PubMed, and PubMed Central. afterlife combines all four into one list, measures how far they agree, and answers lookups for a single paper. It never says a paper is fine. It only says that none of the four lists flags it, as of a given date.</p>\n<h4>How It Works</h4>\n<p>Every night the system downloads each list and stores the raw files untouched, so everything built on top can be rebuilt from them. It matches the same paper across lists by the ID codes that publishers and databases give it. For Retraction Watch, I replayed every saved copy of the full database from September 2024 to October 2026, so I could see each record that changed between copies.</p>\n<p>It runs in a live Amazon Web Services account and on Databricks, a data platform. The Databricks run rebuilt the same history my laptop did. Deploying also found six bugs my local tests had missed.</p>\n<h4>What I Found</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>The lists disagree a lot.</strong> Of 89,681 papers flagged by at least one list, the lists that could have known about a paper all agree on 51.5% of them.</li>\n<li><strong>Half of one list is the announcements.</strong> 49.3% of what OpenAlex marks as retracted are the retraction notices themselves (the short announcements), rather than the papers being retracted. Counted as papers, they would nearly double that list.</li>\n<li><strong>Retracted papers keep getting cited.</strong> Among retracted papers whose whole time since retraction falls inside the citation data, 65.1% were cited again in a later year.</li>\n<li><strong>The source rewrites its own history.</strong> 68.9% of Retraction Watch records were edited after they first appeared, mostly reasons being relabelled. Some changes were not edits at all: one copy was saved in a limited text format that turned unusual characters into question marks. A quality check held that copy back instead of merging it.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>What It Does Not Show</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Combining four lists gives the most generous reading of &ldquo;retracted&rdquo;. It is not a verified true list.</li>\n<li>Citations are counted by year starting in 2012, so the citation figure undercounts, and older retractions are left out of it.</li>\n<li>Papers are matched across lists in a single step, which can miss matches and make the lists look like they agree less than they do.</li>\n<li>It runs in one demo account, not as a production service.</li>\n</ul>", "flow": [{"k": "start", "t": "Which papers are retracted, and according to which registry?", "d": "EvidenceOps blocks retracted papers. afterlife builds one registry from four public ones, measures how far they agree, and serves verdicts that never say &ldquo;ok&rdquo;, only &ldquo;no registry here flags this, as of this date&rdquo;."}, {"k": "split", "t": "Four public retraction registries", "lanes": [[{"k": "data", "t": "Retraction Watch", "d": "Distributed by Crossref. 39 snapshots of the full database, September 2024 to October 2026, replayed from the public GitLab history: 55,804 records growing to 73,283."}], [{"k": "data", "t": "OpenAlex", "d": "136,873 of the 136,970 works it marks retracted. The API prices every call, so the fetcher partitions by publication date and meters its spend: 709 calls, $0.0709."}], [{"k": "data", "t": "PubMed", "d": "35,169 PMIDs carrying the &ldquo;Retracted Publication&rdquo; type."}], [{"k": "data", "t": "PubMed Central", "d": "NCBI&rsquo;s 12.5 million row id map, and 43,616 metadata objects read from the PMC bucket on AWS Open Data."}]]}, {"k": "step", "t": "Immutable landing zone", "d": "Raw bytes land with sha256 manifests, and writes refuse to overwrite. Every layer above rebuilds from these bytes."}, {"k": "check", "t": "Do the Spark identifier jobs match the Python reference rules?", "d": "Identity is resolved in one hop across DOI, PMID, and PMCID. The rules are plain Python; the Spark jobs use native expressions.", "yes": "A test requires agreement on every Retraction Watch, OpenAlex, and PubMed identifier landed. The status rule&rsquo;s closed form also matched the step by step version on all 22,621 notice histories.", "no": "The test fails."}, {"k": "step", "t": "History as a slowly changing dimension cut on meaning", "d": "The 39 Retraction Watch snapshots are replayed in order. A record gets a new version only when its meaning changes, not when its bytes do."}, {"k": "check", "t": "Quality gate: is this snapshot safe to merge?", "d": "Each snapshot is checked before it joins the history.", "yes": "It is merged, and changed records get new versions.", "no": "It is quarantined with its evidence. The 2026-10-15 snapshot was a lossy Windows-1252 export: <code>Wnt/&beta;-catenin</code> became <code>Wnt/?-catenin</code> in 2,298 titles."}, {"k": "result", "t": "68.9% of Retraction Watch records edited after first appearing", "d": "50,518 of 73,334, mostly the reason taxonomy relabelled (&ldquo;Fake Peer Review&rdquo; became &ldquo;Compromised Peer Review&rdquo; on 10,426 records). 3,840 changed in something else, including 96 DOIs and 54 retraction dates. An append only pipeline sees none of it."}, {"k": "result", "t": "Formatting churn is not recorded as edits", "d": "One export dropped a <code>+</code> prefix from every reason label (65,898 records). A history cut on bytes would have recorded edits nobody made."}, {"k": "check", "t": "Do the dbt models give the same rows on DuckDB and Spark SQL?", "d": "One set of SQL, with engine differences kept in dispatch macros.", "yes": "Compared row by row: 210,111 rows, identical.", "no": "A mismatch would point at an engine difference the dispatch macros miss."}, {"k": "step", "t": "CI on two Spark versions", "d": "Its first run caught two bugs: Spark 4 rejects Windows-1252 in the CSV reader, and a date literal only worked on macOS."}, {"k": "result", "t": "All eligible registries agree on 51.5%", "d": "Of 89,681 papers some registry calls retracted, the registries that could know about a paper all agree on 46,225 (51.5%). 36,401 (40.6%) are flagged by exactly one. No registry is complete."}, {"k": "result", "t": "49.3% of OpenAlex&rsquo;s retracted works are notices", "d": "67,544 of 136,873 are retraction notices rather than papers; counted as papers they would nearly double the registry. PMC gets this right: 187 of its 17,951 notice articles carry the flag."}, {"k": "result", "t": "65.1% of retracted papers cited after retraction", "d": "Of 44,557 retracted papers whose whole post retraction period falls inside OpenAlex&rsquo;s citation window, 29,024 were cited in a later year: 217,376 citations. &ldquo;Retract and replace&rdquo; papers are reported separately."}, {"k": "result", "t": "Retraction is slow, and 2023 was paper mills", "d": "Median 493 days from publication to retraction across 67,845 notices; 1,001 days for notices issued so far in 2026. Notices citing &ldquo;Paper Mill&rdquo; went from 4 in 2019 to 6,782 in 2023."}, {"k": "split", "t": "Deployed on two platforms", "lanes": [[{"k": "step", "t": "AWS, defined in Terraform", "d": "S3, Lambda, ECS Fargate, Step Functions, EventBridge Scheduler, DynamoDB, API Gateway, Glue, Athena, KMS, and CloudWatch alarms with a cost budget. A nightly workflow lands each source, and a loader writes 214,646 items into DynamoDB behind a lookup API."}], [{"k": "step", "t": "Databricks Free Edition", "d": "An Asset Bundle deploys a Lakeflow job and pipeline over Unity Catalog, and dbt builds gold on a serverless SQL warehouse."}]]}, {"k": "check", "t": "Does the cloud reproduce the laptop?", "d": "The deployed runs are compared against the laptop&rsquo;s outputs.", "yes": "PubMed landed byte identical on AWS. Databricks rebuilt the same history, 152,336 versions, and an identical export, all 92,097 documents.", "no": "A Spark <code>first()</code> picked a different PMID on Databricks than on the laptop, one of the bugs only deployment found."}, {"k": "result", "t": "Six bugs the local suite had not found", "d": "An AWS Organization policy denying the GitHub OIDC provider, Terraform unable to read <code>aws login</code> credentials, a verdict outside the serving contract, API Gateway collapsing <code>//</code> in DOI URLs, a read only Fargate container, and the Spark <code>first()</code> mismatch."}, {"k": "limit", "t": "The union is generous, not ground truth", "d": "Combining four registries is the most generous reading of &ldquo;retracted&rdquo;."}, {"k": "limit", "t": "Citations counted by year from 2012", "d": "&ldquo;After retraction&rdquo; undercounts, and older retractions are left out of the 65.1% headline."}, {"k": "limit", "t": "One hop identity resolution", "d": "Matching in one hop across DOI, PMID, and PMCID can undercount agreement, so 51.5% may be low."}, {"k": "limit", "t": "One demo account", "d": "Snowflake and BigQuery profiles compile but have not run, and the GitHub deploy role is blocked by the account&rsquo;s policy. This is not a production service."}, {"k": "end", "t": "How to read it", "d": "The four registries disagree widely, OpenAlex mixes notices with papers, and retracted papers keep being cited. The numbers rest on a generous union and one hop matching, run in one demo account."}]}, {"title": "Replicating Dr. GRPO: Accuracy Held, the Length Claims Came Out Reversed", "meta": "Reinforcement Learning &middot; Reproducibility &middot; Sep 2026", "badge": "51.6% vs 51.2% on MATH500 &middot; both length claims reversed &middot; 1 seed, 100 steps", "tags": ["Reinforcement Learning", "GRPO", "PyTorch", "LoRA", "vLLM", "Qwen2.5", "MLX", "Bootstrap Intervals", "Reproducibility"], "body": "<div class=\"metric\">MATH500: Dr. GRPO 51.6% vs GRPO 51.2%, paired +0.4 points [-3.4, +4.0] &nbsp;|&nbsp; Dr. GRPO 99 tokens longer per step [77, 123] &nbsp;|&nbsp; wrong answers 680 vs 545 tokens &nbsp;|&nbsp; 1 seed, 100 steps, 500 paired eval problems &nbsp;|&nbsp; every number traces to runs/kaggle/</div>\n\n<h4>The Problem</h4>\n<p>GRPO is the objective behind DeepSeek-R1-Zero, and <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2503.20783\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Liu et al. 2025</a> argue it has two biases built in. It averages each response's loss over that response's own length, so a long wrong answer is penalised less per token than a short one. It also scales each question's advantages by the standard deviation of the group's rewards. Dr. GRPO deletes both terms. The paper reports three things: response length keeps growing under GRPO but not under Dr. GRPO, Dr. GRPO's wrong answers are substantially shorter, and accuracy does not suffer.</p>\n<p>I rebuilt the comparison on the paper's own Figure 5 setup, at the scale a free GPU allows, to see which of the three claims survive.</p>\n\n<h4>The Setup</h4>\n<p>Qwen2.5-1.5B base, the R1 template, MATH training questions, and a binary reward for a correct answer inside the answer tag. The grader is the authors' own code, vendored unmodified, and the training and eval data are their published files. Each step samples 8 responses for each of 16 questions, 128 in all, and both arms train on the same 16 questions at every step. The two run configs differ in exactly two flags: <code>std_norm</code> and <code>length_norm</code> are on for GRPO and off for Dr. GRPO.</p>\n<p>The model is 1.5B rather than 0.5B because the 0.5B base could not produce the strict answer format on MATH, which leaves GRPO with no reward to learn from. 1.5B is also the model in the paper's figure.</p>\n\n<h4>What Reproduced and What Did Not</h4>\n<p>Every comparison is paired: the same questions in training and the same 500 MATH500 problems in evaluation. Brackets are 95% bootstrap intervals.</p>\n<table>\n  <thead><tr><th>Paper's claim</th><th>This replication</th><th>Verdict</th></tr></thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr><td>Length grows more under GRPO</td><td>Dr. GRPO was 99 tokens longer per step [77, 123], and 239 longer over the last third of training [217, 262]. Length trend: GRPO -0.2 tokens per step, Dr. GRPO +3.0</td><td>reversed</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Dr. GRPO's wrong answers are shorter</td><td>Longer: 680 against 545 tokens. On the 199 problems both arms got wrong, Dr. GRPO ran 120 tokens longer [80, 160]</td><td>reversed</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Dr. GRPO keeps accuracy</td><td>51.6% against 51.2%, paired difference +0.4 points [-3.4, +4.0]</td><td><strong>holds</strong></td></tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n<figure class=\"project-figure\">\n  <img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" src=\"images/drgrpo_01.png\" alt=\"Mean training response length per step for GRPO and Dr. GRPO over 100 steps\">\n  <figcaption>Figure 1. Training response length on the same 16 questions per step, as a 5 step rolling mean. The arms track each other for the first 50 steps. After that GRPO shortens and partly recovers while Dr. GRPO keeps growing.</figcaption>\n</figure>\n<figure class=\"project-figure\">\n  <img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" src=\"images/drgrpo_02.png\" alt=\"GRPO minus Dr. GRPO response length per training step\">\n  <figcaption>Figure 2. GRPO minus Dr. GRPO, per step. Above zero is what the paper predicts. From about step 50 the difference runs below zero and stays there.</figcaption>\n</figure>\n<figure class=\"project-figure\">\n  <img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" src=\"images/drgrpo_03.png\" alt=\"Final eval mean length of correct and wrong answers in each arm, with 95% bootstrap intervals\">\n  <figcaption>Figure 3. Final eval on 500 MATH500 problems with greedy decoding. Dr. GRPO is longer on correct and wrong answers alike.</figcaption>\n</figure>\n\n<h4>Half the Mechanism Shows Up</h4>\n<p>The paper's argument has two sides: GRPO should make correct answers shorter and wrong answers longer. The first side appears clearly, with GRPO's correct answers at 262 tokens against 399 for Dr. GRPO. The second does not.</p>\n<p>Truncation does not explain the gap. 55 of 500 final responses hit the 1024 token cap in each arm, and during training the arms truncated a similar share, 8.2 and 9.2 of 128 responses per step.</p>\n\n<h4>What Is Not Identical To The Paper</h4>\n<p>Every deviation applies to both arms equally, and neither term under test depends on any of them. They still bound how far the result generalises.</p>\n<table>\n  <thead><tr><th></th><th>Paper</th><th>Here</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr><td>Training</td><td>full fine tuning</td><td>LoRA, rank 64 on all 7 projections</td><td>free GPUs have 15 GB</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Learning rate</td><td>1e-6</td><td>5e-5</td><td>LoRA conventionally runs 10x to 100x higher</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Generation budget</td><td>3000 tokens</td><td>1024</td><td>memory and time</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Steps</td><td>several hundred</td><td>100</td><td>time</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Rollout</td><td>8 sequential updates per 1024 responses</td><td>1 on policy update per 128 responses</td><td>laptop scale batch</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Hardware</td><td>8x A100, bf16</td><td>Kaggle 2x T4, fp16</td><td>budget</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Seeds</td><td>3</td><td>1</td><td>stopped at seed 0 by choice</td></tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n\n<h4>Checked Before It Was Trusted</h4>\n<ul>\n  <li>The per token gradient is exactly <code>-A/|o|</code> under GRPO and <code>-A/budget</code> under Dr. GRPO, tested in both the MLX and the PyTorch version of the loss.</li>\n  <li>The PyTorch and MLX losses agree to 1e-6 on identical inputs.</li>\n  <li>Merged weights reproduce the LoRA model's outputs before the sampler uses them.</li>\n  <li>During training, the sampler's and the learner's log probabilities for the same tokens never differed by more than 0.004 nats in a step, so the policy being sampled was the policy being trained.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h4>Limitations</h4>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>One seed.</strong> The bootstrap intervals cover variation across steps and problems, not across seeds. Two runs with identical settings drift apart after the first update, because the fp16 backward pass on a GPU is not bit reproducible. The paper used three seeds. This is the biggest gap, and it is why the result reads as \"not reproduced here\" rather than \"refuted\".</li>\n  <li><strong>Short and small.</strong> 100 LoRA steps at a 1024 token budget. The paper's effect could need longer training, full fine tuning, or room to grow past 1024 tokens.</li>\n  <li><strong>The mean hides structure.</strong> Both arms reach the same accuracy by different routes, and GRPO's length collapse between steps 55 and 80 is unexplained.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h4>Engineering Notes</h4>\n<p>The experiment started on an Apple M5 laptop in MLX and moved to Kaggle when the laptop became unusable during runs. Problems found along the way, each fixed in its own commit:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>A Metal GPU timeout, caused by macOS paging out GPU buffers under memory pressure, fixed by wiring the working set.</li>\n  <li>vLLM on a T4 ran at 350 tokens per second. The cause was per request sampling seeds, not LoRA, and removing them tripled throughput.</li>\n  <li>Building all the merged weights at once ran the sampler GPU out of memory. They now stream one tensor at a time.</li>\n  <li>PyTorch reports bf16 as supported on a T4 by emulating it, so bf16 is keyed on compute capability instead.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p>Code, figures and every run artifact are at <a href=\"https://github.com/TanayPratapSingh/drgrpo-replication\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">github.com/TanayPratapSingh/drgrpo-replication</a>.</p>", "simple": "<h4>What I Reran</h4>\n<p>GRPO is a recipe for training an AI model to solve math problems by reward: the model tries several answers to each question, scores a point for each correct one, and learns from the difference. A 2025 paper by Liu and colleagues argued the recipe has two biases built in. One spreads the penalty for an answer across its length, so a long wrong answer is penalised less per token (a word or piece of a word) than a short one. The paper removed both biases and called the result Dr. GRPO.</p>\n<p>The paper made three claims. Under the original recipe answers keep getting longer, and under Dr. GRPO they do not. Dr. GRPO&rsquo;s wrong answers are much shorter. Accuracy does not suffer. I rebuilt the paper&rsquo;s own comparison, with its model, data, and grading code, at the scale a free cloud GPU allows, to see which claims survive.</p>\n<h4>How I Tested It</h4>\n<p>I trained the same model twice, once with each recipe, for 100 steps. The two settings differ in exactly two switches, and both runs trained on the same questions at every step. Then I tested both on the same 500 math problems, so every comparison is like for like. There is one training run per recipe.</p>\n<h4>What I Found</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Accuracy held.</strong> In one run per recipe, Dr. GRPO scored 51.6% and the original 51.2%, a difference too small to tell apart from chance. The paper&rsquo;s claim survives.</li>\n<li><strong>Both length claims came out the other way.</strong> In my single run, Dr. GRPO wrote the longer answers, 99 tokens longer per training step on average. In the same run, its wrong answers averaged 680 tokens against 545 for the original recipe.</li>\n<li><strong>Half of the paper&rsquo;s explanation showed up.</strong> The paper says the original recipe should make correct answers shorter and wrong answers longer. Its correct answers were shorter. Its wrong answers were not longer.</li>\n<li>Answers cut off at the length limit do not explain the gap. The same number hit the limit under each recipe.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>What It Does Not Show</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>One run per recipe.</strong> The paper used three. Two runs with identical settings drift apart on this hardware, so a second run could look different. That is why I read the length result as &ldquo;not reproduced here&rdquo; rather than &ldquo;refuted&rdquo;.</li>\n<li><strong>Short and small.</strong> 100 training steps, answers capped at 1024 tokens, and a cheaper method that trains a small set of extra weights instead of the whole model. The paper&rsquo;s effect could need longer training, full training, or more room for answers to grow.</li>\n<li><strong>The averages hide structure.</strong> Both recipes reach the same accuracy by different routes, and a sharp drop in answer length under the original recipe between steps 55 and 80 is unexplained.</li>\n</ul>", "flow": [{"k": "start", "t": "Do the Dr. GRPO paper&rsquo;s three claims survive a paired rebuild on free GPUs?", "d": "Liu et al. 2025 argue GRPO has two built in biases, and Dr. GRPO deletes both. Claims: length keeps growing under GRPO but not Dr. GRPO, Dr. GRPO&rsquo;s wrong answers are shorter, and accuracy does not suffer."}, {"k": "data", "t": "The paper&rsquo;s own Figure 5 setup", "d": "Qwen2.5-1.5B base, the R1 template, MATH training questions, and a binary reward for a correct answer inside the answer tag. The authors&rsquo; grader is vendored unmodified, with their published training and eval files."}, {"k": "check", "t": "Can the 0.5B base produce the strict answer format on MATH?", "d": "Without the format there is no reward for GRPO to learn from.", "yes": "The smaller model would have been used.", "no": "It could not, so the replication uses 1.5B, which is also the model in the paper&rsquo;s figure."}, {"k": "split", "t": "Two arms that differ in exactly two flags", "lanes": [[{"k": "step", "t": "GRPO: <code>std_norm</code> and <code>length_norm</code> on", "d": "Each response&rsquo;s loss is averaged over its own length, and each question&rsquo;s advantages are scaled by the standard deviation of the group&rsquo;s rewards."}], [{"k": "step", "t": "Dr. GRPO: both flags off", "d": "Both terms are deleted. Nothing else in the run config differs."}]]}, {"k": "step", "t": "Paired training, 1 seed, 100 steps", "d": "Each step samples 8 responses for each of 16 questions, 128 in all, and both arms train on the same 16 questions. LoRA rank 64, a 1024 token budget, on Kaggle 2x T4 in fp16."}, {"k": "step", "t": "Moved from an M5 laptop in MLX to Kaggle", "d": "Fixes along the way: a Metal GPU timeout from paged out buffers, vLLM at 350 tokens per second until per request sampling seeds were removed (throughput tripled), merged weights streamed one tensor at a time, bf16 keyed on compute capability."}, {"k": "check", "t": "Is the per token gradient exactly what each objective says?", "d": "Tested in both the MLX and the PyTorch version of the loss.", "yes": "It is <code>-A/|o|</code> under GRPO and <code>-A/budget</code> under Dr. GRPO, and the two losses agree to 1e-6 on identical inputs.", "no": "The loss would not implement the terms under test, and the comparison would mean nothing."}, {"k": "check", "t": "Is the policy being sampled the policy being trained?", "d": "Merged weights must reproduce the LoRA model&rsquo;s outputs before the sampler uses them.", "yes": "Sampler and learner log probabilities for the same tokens never differed by more than 0.004 nats in a step.", "no": "Rollouts would come from a different policy than the one being updated."}, {"k": "step", "t": "Paired evaluation on MATH500", "d": "The same 500 problems for both arms, greedy decoding. Brackets are 95% bootstrap intervals."}, {"k": "split", "t": "Three claims, three verdicts (1 seed, 100 steps)", "lanes": [[{"k": "result", "t": "Length grows more under GRPO: reversed", "d": "Dr. GRPO was 99 tokens longer per step [77, 123], and 239 longer over the last third [217, 262]. Length trend: GRPO -0.2 tokens per step, Dr. GRPO +3.0. One seed."}], [{"k": "result", "t": "Dr. GRPO&rsquo;s wrong answers are shorter: reversed", "d": "Longer, 680 against 545 tokens. On the 199 problems both arms got wrong, Dr. GRPO ran 120 tokens longer [80, 160]. One seed."}], [{"k": "result", "t": "Dr. GRPO keeps accuracy: holds", "d": "51.6% against 51.2% on MATH500, paired difference +0.4 points [-3.4, +4.0]. One seed."}]]}, {"k": "result", "t": "The length curves split after step 50", "d": "The arms track each other for the first 50 steps. After that GRPO shortens and partly recovers while Dr. GRPO keeps growing, and GRPO minus Dr. GRPO stays below zero."}, {"k": "check", "t": "Does truncation at the 1024 token cap explain the gap?", "d": "Both arms are compared on how often responses hit the cap.", "yes": "The reversal would be an artifact of the budget.", "no": "55 of 500 final responses hit the cap in each arm, and during training the arms truncated 8.2 and 9.2 of 128 responses per step."}, {"k": "result", "t": "Half the paper&rsquo;s mechanism shows up", "d": "GRPO should make correct answers shorter and wrong answers longer. Correct answers were shorter under GRPO, 262 tokens against 399 for Dr. GRPO. Wrong answers were not longer."}, {"k": "limit", "t": "One seed", "d": "The intervals cover variation across steps and problems, not seeds. Identical runs drift apart because the fp16 backward pass is not bit reproducible. The paper used three seeds, so this reads as &ldquo;not reproduced here&rdquo; rather than &ldquo;refuted&rdquo;."}, {"k": "limit", "t": "Short and small", "d": "100 LoRA steps at a 1024 token budget. The paper&rsquo;s effect could need longer training, full fine tuning, or room to grow past 1024 tokens."}, {"k": "limit", "t": "Deviations from the paper, applied to both arms", "d": "LoRA instead of full fine tuning, learning rate 5e-5 against 1e-6, 1024 tokens against 3000, one on policy update per 128 responses, 2x T4 fp16 against 8x A100 bf16. Neither term under test depends on them, but they bound generalisation."}, {"k": "limit", "t": "The mean hides structure", "d": "Both arms reach the same accuracy by different routes, and GRPO&rsquo;s length collapse between steps 55 and 80 is unexplained."}, {"k": "end", "t": "How to read it", "d": "On one seed and 100 LoRA steps, the accuracy claim held and both length claims came out reversed. That is a failure to reproduce at this scale, not a refutation of the paper."}]}, {"title": "Tool Dependency Graph: Deriving Agent Call Order from 1,391 Tool Schemas", "meta": "Agent Infrastructure &middot; Static Analysis &middot; Sep 2026", "badge": "1,391 tools &middot; 46 typed entities &middot; 3,278 edges &middot; 8 of 8 assertions passing", "tags": ["TypeScript", "JSON Schema", "Agent Tooling", "Tool Orchestration", "Graph Construction", "Static Analysis", "Cytoscape", "Bun", "Data Modeling"], "body": "<div class=\"metric\">1,391 tools across two large toolkits &nbsp;|&nbsp; 46 typed entities &nbsp;|&nbsp; 3,278 edges &nbsp;|&nbsp; 1,768 of 1,770 typed required inputs have a producer &nbsp;|&nbsp; every edge traces to a declared output field</div>\n<h4>Why This Matters</h4>\n<p>Agents fail on tool calls for a boring reason. The call needs an argument the agent has no way to produce. A reply tool wants a <code>thread_id</code>, and nobody has one memorised: it is an opaque handle that only exists because some earlier call returned it. An agent that does not know this ordering either hallucinates the handle or asks the user for something the user cannot know either.</p>\n<p>So the useful artifact is a map of which tools have to run first, and which arguments genuinely have to come from a person. This builds that map from nothing but the tools' JSON Schemas.</p>\n<h4>Why Name Matching Fails</h4>\n<p>The twenty line version matches input parameter names against output field names and draws an edge. It does not work, for three reasons that only appear at catalog scale.</p>\n<p><strong>Generic names collide.</strong> <code>id</code>, <code>name</code>, <code>query</code> and <code>state</code> appear on hundreds of unrelated tools, so name matching wires a mail client to a source control API. Every handle is resolved to a namespaced entity instead, scoped to the service that owns the tool.</p>\n<p><strong>A bare <code>id</code> means nothing.</strong> The most common shape in a real catalog is <code>data.threads[].id</code>. The leaf is <code>id</code>; the meaning lives in the parent segment. Discard the path and the single most important producer in the catalog, the thread listing, becomes invisible. Resolution runs in three tiers: the leaf name, then the nearest meaningful ancestor, then the producing tool's own object.</p>\n<p><strong>Almost every response embeds almost every object.</strong> A workflow run response contains a full repository, so taken literally a cancel tool \"produces\" a repository name. True and useless. Producers are split into primary and incidental by the head noun of the tool's object phrase, and incidental ones are suppressed whenever a primary exists. English compounds are head final, which is what makes this work:</p>\n<table>\n  <tr><th>Slug</th><th>Head noun</th><th>Yields</th></tr>\n  <tr><td>CREATE_A_REPOSITORY_VARIABLE</td><td>variable</td><td>not a repo</td></tr>\n  <tr><td>CREATE_AN_ORGANIZATION_REPOSITORY</td><td>repository</td><td>a repo</td></tr>\n  <tr><td>CREATE_AN_ISSUE_COMMENT</td><td>comment</td><td>not an issue</td></tr>\n</table>\n<p>All three are wrong under token matching, and the first two point in opposite directions despite both containing the word repository.</p>\n<h4>Why the Graph Is Bipartite</h4>\n<p>The natural encoding is an edge from every producing tool to every consuming tool. At this scale it collapses: one entity, the repository owner, is required by hundreds of tools and produced by dozens, so that entity alone emits tens of thousands of edges and the picture says nothing.</p>\n<p>Tools and entities are both nodes instead. A listing tool produces <code>gmail.thread_id</code>; <code>gmail.thread_id</code> is required by the reply tool. Edge count drops from producers times consumers to producers plus consumers, the hub structure becomes legible, and the two hop path is exactly the answer to \"what do I run first\".</p>\n\n<h4>What the Numbers Say, and What They Do Not</h4>\n<p>Of 2,728 required inputs across the catalog, 1,770 resolve to a typed entity, and 1,768 of those have at least one tool that can supply them. The headline is 99.9 percent, and it would be dishonest to leave it there: that is coverage of the inputs that resolved to an entity, not of all required inputs. The honest version is that 65 percent of required inputs are typed handles and almost all of those are satisfiable. The rest are content a person writes, paging knobs, enums, and 568 handle shaped parameters the registry does not cover yet.</p>\n<p>Those 568 are reported rather than hidden. They are how the entities for repository id, project id, runner id and invitation id were found in the first place: the unresolved bucket is the registry's own to do list.</p>\n<p>A slug based inference fallback was built for tools with no output schema. It fired zero times. Real output schemas covered every tool that needed one, so every edge in the graph traces to a declared field rather than a guess.</p>\n<h4>How It Is Checked</h4>\n<p>Eight hand written assertions, run with one command. All eight pass now, and every one of them started out failing. Four were real bugs that only surfaced because the assertions were written against the two dependency shapes the project exists to get right, rather than against whatever the code already did:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>the bare <code>id</code> under <code>threads[]</code>, which hid the most important producer in the catalog</li>\n  <li>cross service producers leaking into a plan, so a mail tool was told to call a source control tool to find an email address</li>\n  <li>requiredness not compounding through an optional parent, so fields inside an optional attachment object were reported as required user input</li>\n  <li>the head noun error above, which credited a tool that creates a variable with creating a repository</li>\n</ul>\n<p>None of the four were visible from reading the code. They appeared the moment the output was compared against a case someone had written down in advance.</p>"}, {"title": "Distributed ViT on CIFAR-10: Proving a Speedup, Then Explaining Why It Vanished", "meta": "Distributed Training &middot; Systems Measurement &middot; Sep 2026", "badge": "98% &rarr; 57% scaling efficiency &middot; cause isolated &middot; 24 measured runs", "tags": ["PyTorch", "DDP", "FSDP", "NCCL", "Mixed Precision", "torch.compile", "Vision Transformer", "CUDA", "Benchmarking", "Apple MPS"], "body": "<div class=\"metric\">2x Tesla T4, NCCL &nbsp;|&nbsp; DDP in fp32: 1.96x at 98% efficiency &nbsp;|&nbsp; identical config in fp16: 1.15x at 57% &nbsp;|&nbsp; cause isolated to a 4 vCPU host &nbsp;|&nbsp; 24 measured runs across 3 hardware groups</div>\n\n          <h4>Why This Matters</h4>\n          <p>\n            Almost every distributed training result you read is unfalsifiable. A repository reports a 1.9x speedup on two GPUs and there is no way to tell whether the two rank job computed the same gradient as the one rank job, whether the baseline was tuned as carefully as the treatment, or whether the number came from a machine doing something else at the time. A speedup you cannot verify is only a claim. This project was built so that every claim it makes is checkable, and its main finding is a regression in its own scaling.\n          </p>\n\n          <h4>What Gets Measured, and Against What</h4>\n          <p>\n            Three hardware groups: an Apple M5 laptop with one GPU, multi process gloo over that laptop's 10 CPU cores, and 2x Tesla T4 on Kaggle with NCCL. Speedup is only ever computed inside one group, because a ratio between a T4 and an M5 is arithmetic, not a measurement. A rank scaling baseline must differ from its run in exactly one property: the rank count. Precision, compilation, gradient accumulation, dataloader worker count, epoch count and dataset fraction all have to match, and the reporting layer enforces that rather than trusting the author. That constraint was added after the dashboard was caught dividing a real data run by a synthetic data baseline, which silently reported 1.11x where the matched comparison is 1.15x.\n          </p>\n\n          <h4>Model and Data</h4>\n          <p>\n            A vision transformer written out rather than imported, 1,806,538 parameters, patch size 4 on 32&times;32 inputs giving 64 tokens plus a class token. It is small on purpose, because the model must not be the bottleneck in a throughput measurement, and at this size the gradient payload for each all reduce is about 7 MB. CIFAR-10, 50,000 train and 10,000 test, 30 epochs, no pretraining.\n          </p>\n          <p>\n            The canonical dataset host was unreachable from the development network, failing with an SSL EOF before any payload arrived. The fallback deliberately does not rebuild the original pickle layout, because those files carry published md5 sums a reconstruction cannot match, and the only way to make that path appear to work would be to disable torchvision's integrity checking. Silencing a checksum to make a download look successful is how results stop being trustworthy. The mirror writes its own format instead, and every run artifact records which source it used.\n          </p>\n\n          <h4>Configuration</h4>\n          <table>\n            <thead><tr><th>Parameter</th><th>Value</th></tr></thead>\n            <tbody>\n              <tr><td>Architecture</td><td>ViT, dim 192, depth 6, heads 3, MLP ratio 2.0</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>Regularization</td><td>Stochastic depth 0.1, label smoothing 0.1, grad clip 1.0</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>Optimizer</td><td>AdamW, betas (0.9, 0.95), no decay on norms, biases, class token, position embedding</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>Schedule</td><td>Linear warmup 2 epochs, then cosine, computed per step</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>Learning rate</td><td>1e-3, scaled linearly by world size</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>Batch size</td><td>128 per rank</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>Precision</td><td>Selected from compute capability, not from <code>is_bf16_supported</code></td></tr>\n              <tr><td>Runtime</td><td>torch 2.10.0+cu128, NCCL on CUDA, gloo on CPU, FileStore rendezvous on macOS</td></tr>\n            </tbody>\n          </table>\n\n          <h4>Correctness Before Speed</h4>\n          <p>\n            DDP averages gradients across ranks, so two ranks each holding batch B must produce the same gradient as one process holding batch 2B. If that identity does not hold, every throughput number in the project describes a system training a different model than its own baseline, and the comparison is void. The suite spawns a real gloo process group and checks it to <code>rtol=1e-4</code>. It ships with a second test that deliberately computes the wrong thing, because a correctness test that cannot fail is decoration rather than evidence.\n          </p>\n\n          <figure class=\"project-figure\">\n            <img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" src=\"images/dvit_03.png\" alt=\"Terminal showing the DDP gradient equivalence test and its inverse guard both passing\">\n            <figcaption>Figure 1. The gradient equivalence test and its inverse guard, run in a real process group. The dashboard is then regenerated from 24 run artifacts.</figcaption>\n          </figure>\n\n          <h4>Scaling on 2x Tesla T4</h4>\n          <table>\n            <thead><tr><th>Run</th><th>Ranks</th><th>Strategy</th><th>Precision</th><th>img/s</th><th>Rank speedup</th><th>Peak MB</th><th>Top-1</th></tr></thead>\n            <tbody>\n              <tr><td><code>t4-ws1-fp32</code></td><td>1</td><td>single</td><td>fp32</td><td>1,853</td><td>baseline</td><td>553</td><td>80.70%</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><code>t4-ws1-amp</code></td><td>1</td><td>single</td><td>fp16</td><td>3,810</td><td>baseline</td><td>347</td><td>80.52%</td></tr>\n              <tr class=\"winner\"><td><code>t4-ws2-ddp-fp32</code></td><td>2</td><td>DDP</td><td>fp32</td><td><strong>3,637</strong></td><td><strong>1.96x (98%)</strong></td><td>561</td><td>80.01%</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><code>t4-ws2-ddp-amp</code></td><td>2</td><td>DDP</td><td>fp16</td><td>4,376</td><td>1.15x (57%)</td><td>354</td><td>79.71%</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><code>t4-ws2-ddp-amp-compiled</code></td><td>2</td><td>DDP</td><td>fp16 + compile</td><td>4,706</td><td>no baseline</td><td>345</td><td>80.09%</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><code>t4-ws2-ddp-amp-ga2</code></td><td>2</td><td>DDP</td><td>fp16, accum 2</td><td>4,393</td><td>no baseline</td><td>361</td><td>80.15%</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><code>t4-ws2-fsdp-amp</code></td><td>2</td><td>FSDP</td><td>fp16</td><td>2,884</td><td>0.76x</td><td>338</td><td>79.41%</td></tr>\n            </tbody>\n          </table>\n          <p>\n            Three predictions were written down before this ran, so they could be scored instead of retrofitted. FSDP was predicted to lose, and it does: 2,884 img/s against DDP's 4,376, buying 5% of peak memory for 34% of throughput. At 1,806,538 parameters there is almost nothing to shard. Mixed precision was predicted to be a large win, and it is: 2.06x at one rank with peak memory falling from 553 MB to 347 MB.\n          </p>\n\n          <h4>The Prediction That Was Wrong</h4>\n          <p>\n            The third prediction said DDP would land near 1.8x rather than 2x, reasoning that a small model makes the all reduce expensive relative to compute. That is backwards. A small model means <em>small gradient tensors</em>, about 7 MB, which DDP overlaps with the backward pass almost entirely. In fp32 it scales at 1.96x, 98% efficiency.\n          </p>\n          <p>\n            The same DDP configuration in fp16 scales at 1.15x, 57% efficiency. Hardware, model and communication volume are identical, and the only thing that changed is that each GPU now computes roughly twice as fast. Optimising one layer moved the bottleneck rather than shortening the job, and scaling efficiency turned out not to be a property of the cluster at all.\n          </p>\n\n          <h4>Isolating the Cause</h4>\n          <p>\n            Two suspects: the NCCL all reduce, and the input pipeline. Raising the dataloader worker count and watching what happens would produce a correlation, not a cause. Instead a synthetic input mode serves pre normalised tensors from a small in memory pool, removing decode, augmentation and worker processes entirely, so two ranks against one rank under it is compute plus communication with nothing else in the measurement.\n          </p>\n          <table>\n            <thead><tr><th>Input path</th><th>1 rank</th><th>2 ranks</th><th>Speedup</th><th>Efficiency</th></tr></thead>\n            <tbody>\n              <tr class=\"winner\"><td><strong>Synthetic, no dataloader</strong></td><td>3,956</td><td><strong>7,580</strong></td><td><strong>1.92x</strong></td><td><strong>96%</strong></td></tr>\n              <tr><td>Real data, 2 workers per rank</td><td>3,462</td><td>4,073</td><td>1.18x</td><td>59%</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>Real data, 8 workers per rank</td><td>3,599</td><td>3,996</td><td>1.11x</td><td>56%</td></tr>\n            </tbody>\n          </table>\n          <p>\n            The collective is innocent. With no dataloader the same configuration scales at 1.92x, 96% efficiency. With real data, throughput never passes about 4,073 img/s no matter how many GPUs or workers are thrown at it. The artifacts record <code>cpu_cores: 4</code>. One T4 in fp16 already consumes 3,956 img/s of synthetic input, so 4 vCPUs cannot feed even one GPU comfortably, let alone two. The second T4 is not slow, it is unfed.\n          </p>\n          <p>\n            Raising workers from 2 to 8 per rank made it <em>worse</em>, 3,996 against 4,073, because 16 worker processes on 4 cores buy context switches and nothing else. The synthetic runs also index a small pool, so their 96% is an upper bound on what fixing the input path could achieve, not a promise.\n          </p>\n\n          <figure class=\"project-figure\">\n            <img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" src=\"images/dvit_01.png\" alt=\"Median throughput for every measured run across three hardware groups\">\n            <figcaption>Figure 2. Median throughput across all measured runs. Synthetic input runs are marked, because the fastest bar is an instrument reading and not a training result.</figcaption>\n          </figure>\n\n          <h4>Precision Advice Does Not Survive a Backend Change</h4>\n          <p>\n            On the T4, enabling mixed precision is a 2.06x win. On Apple silicon the identical flag is a regression: 726 img/s against 1,061 in fp32, a 32% slowdown, because eager mode pays for a cast around every operator and the hardware is already efficient at fp32. Adding <code>torch.compile</code> to that same fp16 configuration reaches 2,361 img/s, 2.23x the fp32 baseline, once the casts are fused into the graph. Wall clock for the full 30 epoch run falls from 24 minutes to 11.\n          </p>\n          <p>\n            A related trap is that on a T4, <code>torch.cuda.is_bf16_supported()</code> returns True. The card is sm_75 and has no hardware bf16, so that True reflects emulation. Trusting it selected an emulated path for every mixed precision run, and torch inductor quietly logged that the card does not support bfloat16 natively while the benchmark carried on reporting the result as mixed precision. Precision is now selected from compute capability, and every artifact records the dtype that was actually used.\n          </p>\n\n          <h4>Accuracy as the Control</h4>\n          <p>\n            Across all seven T4 configurations, top-1 spans 79.41% to 80.70%, and the three Apple silicon runs land within 0.16 points of each other. That spread is the control. These are throughput changes, so accuracy should barely move. If mixed precision or compilation had shifted it materially, the correct output would have been a bug report rather than a results table.\n          </p>\n\n          <figure class=\"project-figure\">\n            <img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" src=\"images/dvit_02.png\" alt=\"Test accuracy by epoch for the full length runs, curves nearly overlapping\">\n            <figcaption>Figure 3. Test accuracy by epoch. The curves overlay because precision and compilation change throughput, not learning. The reporting layer excludes short and synthetic runs from this view.</figcaption>\n          </figure>\n\n          <h4>Nine Bugs, Kept On Purpose</h4>\n          <p>\n            The defect log is the most reusable output of the build, so it ships in the repository rather than being tidied away. Four of the nine came from an API answering a slightly different question than the one being asked: <code>is_bf16_supported</code> counting emulation, torchvision's md5 check rejecting a legitimate mirror, MPS reporting instantaneous memory where a peak was wanted, and <code>sys.argv[0]</code> producing a command that could not reproduce its own run. One was a hang rather than a crash. The default TCP rendezvous is blocked on some hosts and the job waits, which reads exactly like slow training and costs hours before anyone suspects the launcher. Three were wrong numbers on the dashboard, all found by pointing a screen recorder at a page nobody had read closely.\n          </p>\n          <p>\n            That last category is why the honesty rule is now enforced rather than promised. A gate extracts every number carrying a measurement unit from the documentation and fails if it does not round, at the precision it was written with, to an artifact value or to a ratio between two artifact values on the same hardware. It found five unsupported claims the first time it ran.\n          </p>\n\n          <div class=\"callout\">\n            <h4>Operational Reading</h4>\n            <p style=\"margin-bottom: 0;\">\n              The same cluster, model and communication volume delivered 98% efficiency in fp32 (the 1.96x) and 57% in fp16, and the difference had nothing to do with the distributed layer. Anyone tuning NCCL in response to that drop would have spent a week in the wrong subsystem. Scaling efficiency is a property of the ratio between accelerator speed and host speed, and optimising the accelerator is what breaks it. The measurement that settles it took six short runs and one flag.\n            </p>\n          </div>"}, {"title": "ZTF Transient Pipeline: Real Time Astronomical Alert Classification and Warehouse", "meta": "Streaming ML &middot; Analytics Engineering &middot; Jul 2026", "badge": "27,378 real ZTF alerts &middot; multimodal CNN &middot; dbt: 8 models, 21 tests", "tags": ["Python", "Apache Kafka", "Avro", "TensorFlow", "Multimodal CNN", "dbt", "DuckDB", "Apache Airflow", "FastAPI", "Astropy"], "body": "<div class=\"metric\">27,378 real Avro alerts (ZTF, Jul 20 2026) &nbsp;|&nbsp; 500 scored end to end &nbsp;|&nbsp; dbt: 8 models, 21 tests, 32 checks passing &nbsp;|&nbsp; two plane architecture &nbsp;|&nbsp; portable DuckDB to BigQuery / Snowflake</div>\n\n          <h4>Why This Matters</h4>\n          <p>In time domain astronomy the telescope produces far more data than any human can look at, and the overwhelming majority of it is noise. A survey astronomer or alert broker operator needs high purity transient candidates surfaced quickly, and needs to trust that the nightly numbers behind them are correct. Those are two jobs: a fast, accurate model at the front, and a tested, governed data layer behind it. I built ZTF Transient Pipeline to do both in one system. It also maps onto the three roles I am applying for: ML engineer, data engineer, and analytics engineer.</p>\n\n          <h4>The Sequel to ShopFloor</h4>\n          <p>I designed this project to close the three gaps I listed as limitations in ShopFloor AI, and to do it on production shaped infrastructure. Simulated Kafka becomes real Avro alert data flowing over a real Kafka broker. A synthetic benchmark dataset becomes a public survey stream. The single node app gains a real warehouse, a dbt transformation layer, and drift monitoring.</p>\n\n          <h4>The Data</h4>\n          <p>The Zwicky Transient Facility (ZTF) streams over a million optical alerts per night as Avro packets over Kafka. Each packet embeds three small image cutouts (science, template, and difference) plus a table of measured features. The pipeline downloads a night of real archived alerts and replays them through its own Dockerized Kafka broker. The payloads are genuine ZTF data; only the timing is replayed. The README says so plainly. Replaying the archive puts real data through a real broker without needing broker whitelisting on the critical path.</p>\n\n          <h4>Streaming ML Plane</h4>\n          <p>fastavro deserializes the alerts, and Astropy handles the astronomical coordinate and time conventions. A multimodal classifier in TensorFlow and Keras scores each alert: a convolutional branch reads the three image cutouts, a tabular branch reads the measured features, and the two are fused to predict whether an alert is a real transient or an artifact, then a coarse class. Rather than invent an accuracy target, the model is framed against BTSbot, a published survey broker classifier that scores detections from the same image plus feature inputs. A FastAPI service exposes the scorer, and a streaming consumer writes predictions and alert metadata into the warehouse.</p>\n\n          <h4>Analytics Engineering Plane</h4>\n          <p>This half is the SQL and analytics engineering work. A dbt project transforms the raw landed alerts through three layers: staging models that clean and type the raw stream, intermediate models that assemble per object lightcurves using window functions, and mart models including an incremental daily alert summary and a model monitoring rollup. The project ships with 21 data quality tests, source freshness checks, and generated lineage docs. It defaults to DuckDB so anyone can run it with zero cloud setup, and the same models port to BigQuery and Snowflake with only a profile change.</p>\n\n          <h4>Orchestration and Monitoring</h4>\n          <p>An Apache Airflow DAG runs the full nightly batch end to end: download, deserialize, load, transform, and monitor. A Population Stability Index drift monitor watches the prediction distribution over time, so a shift in what the model is seeing surfaces as a number rather than a surprise. Continuous integration through GitHub Actions runs the test suite and a dbt build on every push, and a Codespaces devcontainer lets the whole thing spin up in the browser.</p>\n\n          <h4>Proven on Real Data</h4>\n          <p>I ran the pipeline against a real night of the survey: 27,378 Avro alert packets extracted from July 20, 2026. Of those, 500 alerts were loaded, scored, and rebuilt through the warehouse. A full <code>dbt build</code> returned 8 models and 21 tests with 32 checks passing and zero errors, on real data rather than a seed sample. The repository is public at <a href=\"https://github.com/TanayPratapSingh/ztf-transient-pipeline\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">github.com/TanayPratapSingh/ztf-transient-pipeline</a>.</p>\n\n          <h4>Honest Limitations</h4>\n          <ul>\n            <li><strong>Replay, not live consumption.</strong> Nightly archive replay uses real data, but live streaming from the survey broker needs an IP whitelist, so it is kept as an optional upgrade rather than a dependency.</li>\n            <li><strong>Coarse labels.</strong> The classifier demonstrates the pipeline, not a science grade taxonomy. It is a systems project first.</li>\n            <li><strong>Trial scale compute.</strong> Everything runs single node under Docker Compose, not at production survey volume.</li>\n            <li><strong>Validation against broker labels.</strong> There is no spectroscopic confirmation, and the reference labels are themselves model outputs, so the benchmark is relative rather than ground truth.</li>\n          </ul>\n\n          <div class=\"callout\">\n            <h4>What This Project Demonstrates</h4>\n            <p style=\"margin-bottom: 0;\">One repository covers all three roles. For ML engineering there is a multimodal image plus tabular classifier benchmarked against BTSbot; for data engineering, a real Avro over Kafka ingestion pipeline that Airflow orchestrates nightly; for analytics engineering, a dbt transformation layer with tests, freshness checks, lineage, and warehouse portability.</p>\n          </div>"}, {"title": "Groundwork: RAG Evaluation, Guardrails, and an LLM Gateway on Real Data", "meta": "LLM Platform Engineering &middot; RAG Evaluation &middot; Aug 2026", "badge": "recall@5 0.921 &middot; ECE 0.045 &middot; injection recall 0.884 unseen &middot; 113 tests, CI green", "tags": ["Python", "RAG", "LLM Gateway", "Guardrails", "Calibration (ECE)", "Prompt Injection", "BM25", "FastAPI", "Docker", "Kubernetes", "Responsible AI", "CI/CD"], "body": "<div class=\"metric\">recall@5 0.921 &nbsp;|&nbsp; ECE 0.045 &nbsp;|&nbsp; injection recall 0.884 on an unseen attack corpus &nbsp;|&nbsp; correctly declines 96.2% of unanswerable questions &nbsp;|&nbsp; 11,873 questions evaluated per run &nbsp;|&nbsp; 113 tests, CI green</div>\n\n<h4>Why This Matters</h4>\n<p>Getting a good answer out of an LLM feature once is the easy part. The hard part is knowing whether the answer is good, knowing when the system should refuse, proving the guardrails do not reject a tenth of your legitimate traffic, and being able to say what a change cost you. Groundwork is those four things built around a small retrieval augmented generation system, so they have something concrete to be true about.</p>\n<p><strong>Every number in the project is measured on real, public, human labelled data.</strong> No corpus was written for the demo, no gold answers were authored to be easy, and no attack was invented to be caught. A fetch script downloads five datasets and records a SHA-256 for each in a manifest. It runs with no API key at all, on a deterministic offline reader, so every figure below is reproducible on a laptop for free.</p>\n\n<h4>Four Concerns, One Codebase</h4>\n<table>\n  <thead><tr><th>Concern</th><th>What is implemented</th></tr></thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr><td>LLM and RAG evaluation</td><td>Retrieval metrics, SQuAD official answer metrics, abstention scored as a classifier, confidence calibration with reliability diagrams, a three way retrieval ablation, and a release gate that exits non zero</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Guardrails</td><td>Input and output rails, each scored on a published labelled attack corpus and again on an attack corpus it has never seen</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Secure deployment</td><td>Hashed API keys with constant time comparison, a tamper evident audit hash chain, PII redaction measured per entity type, a threat model, and a hardened container</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>LLM gateway</td><td>One chokepoint for every model call: auth, tier routing, two layer cache, per tenant budgets and rate limits, retries, graceful degradation, full cost accounting</td></tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n\n<h4>The Data</h4>\n<table>\n  <thead><tr><th>Dataset</th><th>Role</th><th>Size</th><th>Licence</th></tr></thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr><td>SQuAD v2.0 dev</td><td>Corpus of 1,204 Wikipedia paragraphs, 11,873 questions, gold answer spans, and 5,945 genuinely unanswerable questions</td><td>4.2 MB</td><td>CC BY-SA 4.0</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>deepset/prompt-injections</td><td>Injection rail precision and recall</td><td>662</td><td>CC BY 4.0</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>jackhhao/jailbreak-classification</td><td>Jailbreak rail</td><td>1,306</td><td>See card</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Lakera/gandalf_ignore_instructions</td><td>Held out attacks, never trained on</td><td>1,000</td><td>MIT</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>ai4privacy/pii-masking-200k</td><td>PII redaction recall per entity type</td><td>1,000</td><td>See card</td></tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n\n<h4>What This Project Demonstrates</h4>\n<div class=\"callout\">\n  <p style=\"margin-bottom: 0;\">Groundwork implements four production concerns that usually get skipped, and measures them rather than asserting them: RAG evaluation with a gate that can fail a build, guardrails with published false positive rates on corpora they have never seen, an LLM gateway that meters every call, and a documented security posture. In all four, <strong>the measurement changed the system.</strong> The full public write up and code are at <a href=\"https://github.com/TanayPratapSingh/Groundwork\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">github.com/TanayPratapSingh/Groundwork</a>.</p>\n</div>"}, {"title": "Replicating BEIR: Reproducing a Retrieval Benchmark and Finding a Silent Truncation Bug", "meta": "Information Retrieval &middot; Reproducibility &middot; Sep 2026", "badge": "6 of 6 published cells reproduced &middot; 4 exact &middot; silent truncation bug found and fixed", "tags": ["Python", "Information Retrieval", "BEIR", "BM25", "Dense Retrieval", "sentence-transformers", "DPR", "TAS-B", "pytrec_eval", "Reciprocal Rank Fusion", "Reproducibility"], "body": "<div class=\"metric\">6 of 6 published cells reproduced &nbsp;|&nbsp; 4 exact to three decimals &nbsp;|&nbsp; DPR on SciFact recovered from 0.260 to a published 0.318 &nbsp;|&nbsp; 12 minutes on a laptop, no GPU &nbsp;|&nbsp; every number traces to an artifact in runs/</div>\n\n<h4>Why This Matters</h4>\n<p>Reading a paper tells you what the authors claim. Rebuilding it tells you whether the claim survives contact with a different machine, a different implementation and a fresh set of defaults. I rebuilt the central experiment from <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2104.08663\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">BEIR</a> (Thakur et al., NeurIPS 2021 Datasets and Benchmarks) from scratch: the claim that dense retrievers trained on MS MARCO lose to a plain bag of words baseline once you move them to a domain they were not trained on.</p>\n<p>The useful part of a replication is the one cell that disagrees, because chasing it is the only way to learn something the paper did not tell you.</p>\n\n<h4>What Reproduced</h4>\n<p>I used two scientific datasets from the benchmark, chosen because they are the smallest and therefore fit on a laptop: SciFact (5,183 documents, 300 queries) and NFCorpus (3,633 documents, 323 queries). Both counts match the paper exactly. The metric is nDCG@10, scored with pytrec_eval, the same trec_eval bindings the authors report with.</p>\n<table>\n  <thead><tr><th>System</th><th>SciFact published</th><th>SciFact mine</th><th>NFCorpus published</th><th>NFCorpus mine</th></tr></thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr><td>BM25</td><td>0.665</td><td>0.677</td><td>0.325</td><td>0.320</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>TAS-B</td><td>0.643</td><td><strong>0.643</strong></td><td>0.319</td><td><strong>0.319</strong></td></tr>\n    <tr><td>DPR</td><td>0.318</td><td><strong>0.318</strong></td><td>0.189</td><td>0.188</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>bge-small (2023, not in the paper)</td><td>n/a</td><td>0.713</td><td>n/a</td><td>0.343</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Hybrid RRF (not in the paper)</td><td>n/a</td><td>0.687</td><td>n/a</td><td>0.341</td></tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The paper holds for the models the paper tested. It does not survive a 2023 embedder: bge-small-en-v1.5, at 33M parameters, beats BM25 on both datasets. The authors were right in 2021 and the gap they identified has since closed. Reciprocal rank fusion over BM25 and TAS-B also beat both of its own inputs on both datasets, and took the best Recall@100 on SciFact at 0.948.</p>\n\n<h4>The Cell That Disagreed</h4>\n<p>DPR on SciFact came in at 0.260 against a published 0.318. Not a rounding difference. TAS-B had already reproduced exactly on both datasets, so the loader, the metrics and the scoring were not at fault, which left something specific to how DPR sees a document.</p>\n<p>The cause is a default. The sentence-transformers port of the DPR context encoder ships with <code>max_seq_length = 100</code>, so every abstract was cut off after roughly its first paragraph. No error, no warning, no sign of it in the output. The tell was that searching with <strong>only the title</strong> scored 0.261 against title plus full text at 0.260. That only makes sense if the body was being discarded.</p>\n<table>\n  <thead><tr><th>Context encoder token limit</th><th>nDCG@10</th><th>Recall@100</th></tr></thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr><td>100 (the shipped default)</td><td>0.276</td><td>0.601</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>256</td><td>0.298</td><td>0.687</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>350</td><td>0.315</td><td>0.710</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>512</td><td><strong>0.318</strong></td><td><strong>0.727</strong></td></tr>\n    <tr><td>published</td><td>0.318</td><td>0.727</td></tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The bug was invisible on NFCorpus, which reproduced either way. NFCorpus carries 38.2 judged documents per query against SciFact’s 1.1, so it absorbs ranking damage that SciFact exposes. Run on NFCorpus alone, this replication would have hidden the bug entirely. A benchmark with one dataset cannot tell you when you are wrong.</p>\n\n<h4>What Is Not Identical To The Paper</h4>\n<p>Every reproduction deviates somewhere. Stating the deviations is the difference between a replication and a claim.</p>\n<table>\n  <thead><tr><th>Deviation</th><th>Why, and what it costs</th></tr></thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr><td>BM25 is not Anserini</td><td>The paper scores BM25 with Anserini, which is Lucene. That needs a Java runtime the machine did not have, so BM25 is reimplemented in Python with the same k1 of 0.9 and b of 0.4, the same Porter stemming and the same 33 word Lucene English stopword list, but one concatenated field instead of two and exact rather than quantised document lengths. This is the likely source of the only two inexact cells, +0.012 and -0.005.</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Plain rather than capped recall</td><td>The paper reports capped Recall@100. The two agree for every SciFact query but not for every NFCorpus query.</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>bge-small is an addition</td><td>A 2023 model the paper could not have tested, included to measure how far the field moved, and labelled as not in the paper wherever it appears.</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Hybrid RRF is an addition</td><td>Reciprocal rank fusion with k of 60, from Cormack et al. 2009.</td></tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n\n<h4>What This Project Demonstrates</h4>\n<div class=\"callout\">\n  <p style=\"margin-bottom: 0;\">That I can take a published result, rebuild it independently, and say precisely where my numbers agree and where they do not. The six matching cells were worth less than the one that did not match, because chasing it surfaced a <strong>library default that silently discards most of every document</strong> with no error and no warning. The same failure mode is waiting in any retrieval system whose chunks are longer than an embedding model’s configured limit, which is most of them. Every number here traces to a command that was run and a JSON artifact committed under <code>runs/</code>. Code at <a href=\"https://github.com/TanayPratapSingh/beir-replication\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">github.com/TanayPratapSingh/beir-replication</a>.</p>\n</div>"}, {"title": "JMA Wireless: Standardizing Five Factory KPIs and Locating the Real Constraint", "meta": "Lean Six Sigma &middot; Analytics Engineering &middot; Jan to May 2026", "badge": "project lead, 4 person team &middot; 31 GB JSON to under 5 MB &middot; ~$355K projected annualized opportunity", "tags": ["DMAIC", "Power BI", "DAX", "Python", "Pandas", "openpyxl", "ETL", "Minitab", "SIPOC", "Pugh Matrix", "FMEA", "Control Plan"], "body": "<div class=\"metric\">5 KPIs standardized across 2 lines &nbsp;|&nbsp; 31 GB of nested PLC JSON reduced to under 5 MB &nbsp;|&nbsp; automated line at 97.3% uptime but 46.3% schedule attainment &nbsp;|&nbsp; ~$355K projected annualized opportunity &nbsp;|&nbsp; 15 week DMAIC engagement</div>\n\n<h4>Why This Matters</h4>\n<p>JMA Wireless builds cell tower transmitters and active 5G infrastructure in Liverpool, New York. Its Jumper Department produces the radio frequency jumper cables that connect transmitters to the antennas on towers and small cells. The parts are functionally simple and precision sensitive: every connector must be terminated to tight torque and electrical specification, and every cable must pass continuity, return loss, and visual inspection before it ships.</p>\n<p>The department runs two parallel lines with completely different economics. Jumper 1.0 is manual, staffed by eight operators across two ten hour shifts. Jumper 2.0 is a fully automated cell with PLC level data capture. Before this project there was no way to compare them, because terms like Uptime and Schedule Attainment meant different things to different functions. Leadership could not draw a clean line from what happened on the floor on Tuesday to the financial plan.</p>\n\n<h4>What I Owned</h4>\n<p>I was Project Lead on a four person Whitman School team running a full DMAIC engagement from January to May 2026, sponsored by JMA’s SVP of Global Operations with a named project champion and weekly on site access. I wrote the charter, SIPOC, and CTQC tree, ran the KPI definition interviews, built the Python pipeline that made the automated line’s data usable, and wrote the Control Plan that governs the dashboard after handover.</p>\n\n<h4>The Method</h4>\n<p>\n  DMAIC as it was actually run, including the loops that fired. Two of them mattered. A root cause that did not survive validation went back for re analysis, and a target the data showed was unreachable went back to be revalidated rather than left red on a dashboard forever.\n</p>\n\n<figure class=\"project-figure\">\n  <img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" src=\"images/jma_02.jpg\" alt=\"DMAIC project flow from problem identification through KPI definition, baseline, root cause analysis, solution selection, dashboard build, control plan and monitoring\">\n  <figcaption>Figure 1. The DMAIC path run on this engagement, including the re analysis and target validation loops</figcaption>\n</figure>\n\n<h4>Standardizing the KPIs</h4>\n<p>\n  The most important Define deliverable was a written, sponsor approved definition document: five KPIs, each with one agreed formula and one agreed target, confirmed by the sponsor before anything was built.\n</p>\n<table>\n  <thead><tr><th>KPI</th><th>Formula</th><th>Target</th></tr></thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr><td>Parts per Labor Hour</td><td>Total good parts / total labor hours</td><td>&ge; 50</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Uptime</td><td>Runtime / (runtime + downtime)</td><td>&ge; 85%</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>First Time Throughput</td><td>Good parts / total parts tested</td><td>&ge; 95%</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Schedule Attainment</td><td>Actual parts / weekly target</td><td>&ge; 100%</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Culture and Engagement</td><td>Pulse score (60%) + weekly active dashboard users (40%)</td><td>Index &ge; 75</td></tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n<p>\n  The Culture and Engagement metric is the unusual one. Both JMA and Whitman treat culture as a real lean variable, but standard manufacturing data never captures it, so I specified a five question two minute pulse survey plus a passive count of distinct named users who open the dashboard at least twice a week.\n</p>\n\n<h4>The Data Engineering Problem</h4>\n<p>\n  The automated cell publishes one JSON record per test event, and each record nests an array of tests, each nesting an array of measurements, each nesting an array of channel readings. Two raw datasets of 809 and 1,000 records expanded to more than 31 GB when loaded straight into Power BI. That is unusable twice over: Power BI expands the nesting on every visual that touches the data, and nobody with less than 64 GB of RAM can open the file at all.\n</p>\n<p>\n  I built a Python pipeline with pandas and openpyxl that walks the nesting with explicit <code>json_normalize</code> record paths and writes one flat row per test event. It casts timestamps to datetime, channel readings to float, and pass and fail to boolean, so Power Query never has to coerce anything. It also computes cycle time in seconds as a direct input to the Uptime and Parts per Labor Hour measures. The output is under 5 MB and loads instantly.\n</p>\n<table>\n  <thead><tr><th>Stage</th><th>Action</th></tr></thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr><td>Extract</td><td>Load raw test report JSON</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Parse</td><td>Walk the nesting with explicit <code>json_normalize</code> record paths</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Type cast</td><td>datetime64, float64, boolean, so Power BI needs no coercion</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Calculate</td><td>Derive cycle time in seconds from start and end timestamps</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Load</td><td>Write a Power BI ready workbook via openpyxl</td></tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n\n<h4>Baseline: What the Standardized Numbers Showed</h4>\n<table>\n  <thead><tr><th>KPI</th><th>Jumper 1.0 (manual)</th><th>Jumper 2.0 (automated)</th><th>Target</th></tr></thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr><td>First Time Throughput</td><td>87.9%</td><td>92.5%</td><td>95%</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Uptime</td><td>31.3%</td><td><strong>97.3%</strong></td><td>85%</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Schedule Attainment</td><td>139.7%</td><td><strong>46.3%</strong></td><td>100%</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Parts per Labor Hour</td><td>34.91</td><td>7.94</td><td>50</td></tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n<p>\n  Parts per Labor Hour is the one number on that table that should not be read straight across. Both lines use the same scheduled labor hours denominator so the comparison is consistent, but the automated cell only needs a supervising technician for part of a shift. Measured on engaged technician minutes, the automated figure is roughly an order of magnitude higher, so the dashboard exposes both that figure and a cell specific cycles per engaged hour measure rather than letting 7.94 stand as a verdict.\n</p>\n\n<h4>The Finding That Mattered</h4>\n<p>\n  The automated cell held <strong>97.3% uptime</strong> and still delivered only <strong>46.3% of its weekly target</strong>. Those two numbers cannot both be a machine problem. A cell that runs almost all of the time and still misses plan by more than half is input limited rather than equipment limited. The binding constraint was what entered the cell, not the cell itself.\n</p>\n<p>\n  The manual line was quietly absorbing the shortfall at 139.7% of its own target. That looks like heroics and reads well on a weekly report, but it depresses labor productivity, strains the operator pool, and masks the real demand signal going back to planning. The pattern only became visible because standardizing the definitions made the two numbers comparable.\n</p>\n<p>\n  A fishbone on that gap put the leading causes in Method, Material, and Measurement: kitting batches sized for the manual line, connector subassemblies arriving batched rather than continuously, and a weekly target set at commissioning that had never been revisited against demonstrated capability.\n</p>\n\n<h4>Where Quality and Time Actually Go</h4>\n<p>\n  Two Paretos carried the Analyze phase. On quality, torque and return loss together account for roughly three quarters of all first time failures, which points at tooling and torque training on one side and connector seating on the other, not at a diffuse quality problem.\n</p>\n\n<figure class=\"project-figure\">\n  <img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" src=\"images/jma_01.jpg\" alt=\"Pareto chart of first time throughput failure modes on Jumper 1.0: torque out of spec 41 percent, return loss 33 percent, visual reject 14 percent, continuity fail 8 percent, other 4 percent\">\n  <figcaption>Figure 2. FTT failure modes on Jumper 1.0. Torque and return loss are 74% of all first time failures</figcaption>\n</figure>\n\n<p>\n  On time, the manual line loses more than a third of its scheduled hours waiting for material and another quarter to changeover. That is the same upstream flow problem that starves the automated cell, in a different shape.\n</p>\n<table>\n  <thead><tr><th>Jumper 1.0 downtime category</th><th>Share</th><th>Typical event</th><th>Per week</th></tr></thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr><td>Material wait (cable, connectors)</td><td><strong>38%</strong></td><td>18 min</td><td>~24</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Setup and changeover</td><td>27%</td><td>32 min</td><td>~9</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Tool calibration and maintenance</td><td>19%</td><td>22 min</td><td>~9</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Operator absent or break overrun</td><td>12%</td><td>11 min</td><td>~11</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Other</td><td>4%</td><td>mixed</td><td>mixed</td></tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n\n<h4>Recommendations, and the One I Piloted</h4>\n<p>\n  Three recommendations came out of Improve, selected against six candidates with a Pugh matrix and sequenced on a benefit and effort grid: a fifteen minute daily standup held at a wall mounted screen showing the dashboard, a two bin continuous kitting feed so the automated cell is never starved of inputs, and a reset of the cell’s weekly target from the number set at commissioning to demonstrated capability, with a documented ramp as kitting improves.\n</p>\n<p>\n  The third one is worth defending because it looks like moving the goalposts. A target the cell cannot currently hit makes the dashboard permanently red, and a permanently red dashboard is one people stop reading. Resetting it to roughly 1,150 cables per week and ramping by about 200 per week as the kitting fix lands means the team can see progress it is actually making.\n</p>\n<p>\n  I piloted the standup for two weeks on the manual line first shift and measured it rather than asserting it worked. Median time from a KPI going red to a named owner taking the first action fell from about <strong>4 hours to about 25 minutes</strong>, because the standup surfaced the red status instead of a supervisor finding it on a walk. Operator self reported familiarity with the five KPIs rose from <strong>2.4 to 4.1</strong> on a five point scale.\n</p>\n\n<h4>What the Dollar Figure Rests On</h4>\n<p>\n  The headline number is a projection, not money in the bank. Closing the automated line’s schedule attainment gap to plan recovers roughly <strong>27,000 cables per year</strong> with no added labor. At an assumed <strong>$12 contribution margin per cable</strong> that is about $324,000 a year. Retiring the manual weekly reporting cycle frees about <strong>48 analyst hours per week</strong>, roughly $31,200 a year at an assumed <strong>$12.50 fully loaded hourly rate</strong>. Together that is about $355,000 of recurring annual opportunity.\n</p>\n<p>\n  Both of those rates are conservative assumptions the team adopted pending confirmation from JMA Finance, and the report states them in an assumptions table so any figure can be revised without re deriving the analysis. The cable recovery is a projection from demonstrated capability, not an observed post implementation result.\n</p>\n\n<h4>What I Would Do Differently</h4>\n<p>\n  The Culture and Engagement metric was scoped late and is the one KPI with no baseline value; the instrument is built and wired into the dashboard but the first administration slipped past the engagement. I also did not write unit tests for the Python pipeline. It produces correct output today, but the only thing standing between a schema change upstream and a silently wrong dashboard is an analyst noticing, and a small set of assertions would have removed that dependency before handover.\n</p>\n\n<h4>Sustainment</h4>\n<p>\n  The dashboard ships with a Control Plan rather than a handshake: a named refresh owner and cadence, an FMEA on the dashboard itself covering the ways a stale or broken feed could mislead a supervisor, and a tiered escalation framework that triggers on red KPI status so a red light has a defined next action instead of being ambient.\n</p>\n\n<h4>What This Project Demonstrates</h4>\n<div class=\"callout\">\n  <p style=\"margin-bottom: 0;\">The engineering and the judgement both had to be right. The pipeline work was real (31 GB of nested PLC JSON is not a dataset you can wish into Power BI), but the finding that mattered came from definitions, not code: once Uptime and Schedule Attainment meant the same thing on both lines, <strong>97.3% uptime against 46.3% attainment</strong> located the constraint upstream of the machine everyone assumed was the problem. The standup pilot was tested and reported rather than claimed, and the projection is labelled as a projection even though a bigger number was available for the taking.</p>\n</div>"}, {"title": "ShopFloor AI: Predictive Maintenance and RAG for the Factory Floor", "meta": "MLOps &middot; Predictive Maintenance &middot; Jul 2026", "badge": "0.85 F1 &middot; 0.96 AUC &middot; 7-service deployed stack", "tags": ["Python", "Scikit learn", "Gradient Boosting", "MLflow", "Apache Kafka", "Apache Airflow", "Docker Compose", "FastAPI", "Streamlit", "LangChain", "ChromaDB", "RAG"], "body": "<div class=\"metric\">Gradient Boosting F1 = 0.85 &nbsp;|&nbsp; AUC-ROC = 0.96 &nbsp;|&nbsp; 3 models tracked in MLflow &nbsp;|&nbsp; 16 engineered features &nbsp;|&nbsp; 7-service Docker Compose stack &nbsp;|&nbsp; AI4I 2020 (UCI, CC BY 4.0)</div>\n\n          <h4>Overview</h4>\n          <p>ShopFloor AI is an end to end manufacturing platform. It predicts machine failures from sensor data with classical ML, streams real time production KPIs through Apache Kafka, and answers natural language questions over maintenance documentation with LLM powered retrieval augmented generation. The three parts run as seven containerized services rather than a notebook. The data is the AI4I 2020 Predictive Maintenance Dataset (UCI ML Repository, CC BY 4.0).</p>\n\n          <h4>Tech Stack</h4>\n          <table>\n            <thead><tr><th>Layer</th><th>Technology</th><th>Purpose</th></tr></thead>\n            <tbody>\n              <tr><td>Streaming</td><td>Apache Kafka</td><td>Real time sensor ingestion and anomaly alerting</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>ML tracking</td><td>MLflow</td><td>Experiment logging, model comparison, registry</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>Orchestration</td><td>Apache Airflow</td><td>Scheduled retraining with data quality gates</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>RAG</td><td>LangChain + ChromaDB + OpenAI API</td><td>Natural language Q&amp;A over SOPs</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>API</td><td>FastAPI</td><td>REST endpoints for predictions and queries</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>Dashboard</td><td>Streamlit + Plotly</td><td>KPI monitoring, failure predictor, chat, drift</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>Infrastructure</td><td>Docker Compose</td><td>7 containerized microservices</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>ML</td><td>scikit-learn</td><td>Gradient Boosting / Random Forest classifiers</td></tr>\n            </tbody>\n          </table>\n\n          <h4>How It Works</h4>\n          <p>A Streamlit dashboard calls a FastAPI backend, which fronts three subsystems: an MLflow model registry for predictions, a ChromaDB vector store queried through LangChain and the OpenAI API for document Q&amp;A, and a Kafka broker carrying the live sensor stream. A producer replays sensor data onto the topic and a consumer computes rolling KPIs and raises anomaly alerts. An Airflow DAG retrains the model nightly behind data quality gates.</p>\n          <pre>Streamlit dashboard\n     |  HTTP\nFastAPI backend  -->  /predict  ·  /ask  ·  /health\n     |-- MLflow model registry\n     |-- ChromaDB vector DB  -->  LangChain + OpenAI  (RAG)\n     +-- Kafka broker  -->  producer (sensor stream) + consumer (rolling KPIs, alerts)\n\nAirflow (daily, 2 AM):  data-quality gate --> train --> evaluate --> promote --> RAG refresh</pre>\n\n          <h4>Dataset</h4>\n          <p>AI4I 2020 Predictive Maintenance Dataset (UCI, CC BY 4.0): 10,000 records at a 3.39% failure rate, across five independent failure modes (tool wear, heat dissipation, power, overstrain, random). Raw signals include air and process temperature, rotational speed, torque, tool wear, and a product quality variant (L 50% / M 30% / H 20%).</p>\n\n          <h4>Feature Engineering</h4>\n          <p>16 features are derived from the 5 raw sensor readings so the physics of failure is explicit rather than latent. Representative examples:</p>\n          <table>\n            <thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Derivation</th><th>Purpose</th></tr></thead>\n            <tbody>\n              <tr><td><code>temp_diff</code></td><td>Process temp &minus; Air temp</td><td>Heat dissipation indicator</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><code>power_W</code></td><td>Torque &times; (2&pi; &times; RPM / 60)</td><td>Power failure detection</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><code>wear_torque_product</code></td><td>Tool wear &times; Torque</td><td>Overstrain indicator</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><code>tool_wear_ratio</code></td><td>Tool wear / 240</td><td>Normalized wear proximity to failure</td></tr>\n            </tbody>\n          </table>\n\n          <h4>Results</h4>\n          <table>\n            <thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>F1</th><th>AUC-ROC</th></tr></thead>\n            <tbody>\n              <tr class=\"winner\"><td><strong>Gradient Boosting</strong></td><td><strong>0.8548</strong></td><td><strong>0.9591</strong></td></tr>\n              <tr><td>Random Forest</td><td>0.8387</td><td>0.9823</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>Logistic Regression</td><td>0.3054</td><td>0.9455</td></tr>\n            </tbody>\n          </table>\n          <p>Gradient Boosting was selected and registered in MLflow as the production model. A newly trained model must clear a 0.80 F1 threshold in the nightly pipeline before it can be promoted, so a bad batch never reaches production.</p>\n\n          <h4>Setup</h4>\n          <p>The whole system runs end to end with Docker Compose behind a Makefile:</p>\n          <pre>git clone https://github.com/TanayPratapSingh/shopfloor-ai\ncd shopfloor-ai\ncp .env.example .env      # add your OPENAI_API_KEY\nmake setup                # install deps, download AI4I data, build images\nmake train                # train 3 models, log to MLflow, register the best\nmake up                   # start all 7 services\nmake ingest               # load 6 SOPs into ChromaDB for RAG</pre>\n          <p>Services come up on these ports: dashboard (:8501), API docs (:8001/docs), MLflow (:5001), Airflow (:8080). A 15-test suite (<code>make test</code>) and GitHub Actions CI cover data loading, feature engineering, streaming, and SOP structure.</p>\n\n          <h4>Limitations and Future Work</h4>\n          <ul>\n            <li><strong>Synthetic dataset.</strong> AI4I is 10,000 synthetic records; a production deployment would integrate real MES/SCADA feeds and far larger volumes.</li>\n            <li><strong>Replayed streaming.</strong> The Kafka pipeline replays historical records rather than consuming a live feed; the architecture supports real connectors.</li>\n            <li><strong>Bundled SOPs.</strong> RAG answers depend on the 6 shipped documents; organization specific docs would improve retrieval relevance.</li>\n            <li><strong>Planned.</strong> SHAP explanations in the prediction API, multi model A/B testing in the DAG, WebSocket live dashboard updates, and AWS ECS deployment with S3 backed MLflow storage.</li>\n          </ul>\n\n          <div class=\"callout\">\n            <h4>What This Project Demonstrates</h4>\n            <p style=\"margin-bottom: 0;\">The interesting engineering is everything around the model. A nightly retraining schedule runs behind data quality gates, a registry separates trained models from deployed ones, a streaming layer keeps predictions current, and a RAG assistant answers from real SOPs.</p>\n          </div>"}, {"title": "handoff: Supervisor Orchestration with Escalations That Have Deadlines", "meta": "Agent Orchestration &middot; Human in the Loop &middot; Sep 2026", "badge": "212 tests &middot; zero dependencies &middot; unanswered page &rarr; safer substitute", "tags": ["Python", "Agent Orchestration", "Supervisor", "Human in the Loop", "Escalation Policy", "State Machine", "Audit Trail", "Zero Dependencies", "unittest", "CI/CD"], "body": "<div class='metric'>212 tests &middot; 11 behavioural scenarios &middot; zero dependencies &middot; unanswered page &rarr; safer substitute &middot; CI green on Python 3.11 to 3.13</div>\n\n<h4>Why This Matters</h4>\n<p>Most human in the loop is a modal dialog that blocks forever. The agent reaches something risky, calls confirm(), and waits. It does not say who is being asked, it does not say how long they have, and it has no answer at all for the case that actually happens at 3am, which is that nobody replies. An agent parked on an unanswered prompt is worse than one that never asked, because the incident is still burning and now nothing is watching it. handoff treats escalation as a routing problem with tiers, skills, load, deadlines and a written down behaviour for silence, and builds the rest of the system around that.</p>\n\n<h4>The Sequel to comms-agent</h4>\n<p>comms-agent put a human in the loop gate inside an agent graph: flag the low confidence items, let a reviewer correct them, then report. That answers what happens when the reviewer shows up. handoff covers what happens when they do not, which is the case that breaks these systems in production.</p>\n\n<h4>Escalation as Policy, Not a Callback</h4>\n<p>The policy is a table keyed by risk, held as data in config/policy.yaml: which tiers may approve, in what order, how many seconds the first tier gets, how many signatures are required, and what happens on expiry. Because it is data, somebody who does not read Python can review it in a pull request. Four classes of mistake are caught at load time rather than during an incident: a rule that requires approval without stating an expiry behaviour, a quorum larger than its own ladder, a ladder tier that no responder stands on, and a declared fallback that is missing, self referential, or not actually safer.</p>\n\n<h4>When Nobody Answers</h4>\n<p>An irreversible failover_database is proposed. The page goes to the engineer on call who holds the infrastructure skill, with 180 seconds to answer. Nobody answers. The system neither fails over nor sits still. It runs enable_read_only instead, the substitute the failover tool itself declares. Writes are rejected and checkout degrades, but no transaction in flight is lost and a human can reverse it on arrival. The journal records that it happened because nobody answered, which is a different fact from a human deciding it was a good idea.</p>\n\n<h4>Disagreement Is a Second Trigger</h4>\n<p>Risk is not the only reason to involve a person. When the log analyst sees a high error rate and the metrics analyst sees healthy latency, both confidently, the hypothesis is marked contested rather than averaged into a clean answer the system does not actually have. Any action that addresses a contested hypothesis has its effective risk raised one level, so a low risk service restart that would normally run unattended stops and asks. Uncertainty about the diagnosis and danger in the fix are different problems with the same remedy, and one mechanism means nobody has to remember to wire up a second check.</p>\n\n<h4>What It Refuses To Do</h4>\n<p>The expiry behaviours are deny, safe_fallback, escalate and hold. There is deliberately no allow. Approving an action because nobody looked at it in time is not approval; it is the absence of a decision written down as though it were one. A ladder whose terminal state can be \"ran it anyway\" is a delay mechanism wearing the costume of a control. A test asserts that the enum member does not exist, so adding it means deleting a test whose docstring explains why it should not.</p>\n\n<h4>Honest Limitations</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>The cluster is simulated.</strong> It is a dataclass. Nothing in the project has talked to Kubernetes, a database, or a real pager.</li>\n<li><strong>Rules, not models.</strong> The five workers are deterministic with hand written thresholds, and no LLM is called anywhere. Whether the escalation layer holds up when the workers are nondeterministic is the open question this project does not answer.</li>\n<li><strong>No scheduler.</strong> The caller drives the deadline tick, which is what makes every deadline testable at its precise boundary. It also means a deployment needs something that calls it on a timer, and that part is not written here.</li>\n<li><strong>No notification channel.</strong> The journal exposes a subscribe hook where a pager would attach. Nothing attaches to it.</li>\n<li><strong>Static roster.</strong> Seven people in a YAML file, with no rotation schedule, timezones, or handover.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<div class='callout'>\n<h4>What This Project Demonstrates</h4>\n<p style='margin-bottom: 0;'>In handoff the supervision is the product rather than a wrapper around it. A supervisor is the only component permitted to execute. Workers are handed a registry filtered to read only tools, so they cannot reach a mutating one by name. The escalation layer has tiers, skills, load balancing, quorum, deadlines and a declared behaviour for silence. There are no dependencies, so the whole suite runs on a bare interpreter in under a tenth of a second. During the build the suite caught two design flaws, one of which was a safety path that could never actually run.</p>\n</div>"}, {"title": "comms-agent: Agent Orchestration for Communications Monitoring", "meta": "Agent Orchestration &middot; LLM Systems &middot; Aug 2026", "badge": "stateful graph &middot; tool calling &middot; 9 of 24 flagged, 7 corrected via HITL gate", "tags": ["Python", "Agent Orchestration", "State Graph", "Tool Calling", "LLM", "Human in the Loop", "Reporting"], "body": "<div class='metric'>24 mention run &middot; 9 flagged low-confidence &middot; 7 corrected by human before reporting &middot; plan &rarr; classify &rarr; HITL gate &rarr; report</div>\n\n<h4>Why This Matters</h4>\n<p>This is the automation layer that sits on top of a monitoring pipeline. Where media-pulse is the measurement system, comms-agent runs it and produces the brief. It targets three qualifications that come up together: agent orchestration with planning and tool calling, AI driven automation combined with human quality assurance, and responsible AI practice through an explicit confidence gate and an audit trail.</p>\n\n<h4>The Agent</h4>\n<p>The flow runs plan, ingest, classify, a conditional review gate, human review, aggregate, and report. The gate is a conditional edge. If any mention came back below the confidence threshold, the run routes to human review; otherwise it skips straight to aggregation. So the human in the loop quality gate lives in the agent's control flow. On the sample run the agent flags 9 of 24 mentions and the reviewer, simulated in the demo, corrects 7 of them before anything is reported, so the brief is built from the corrected labels.</p>\n\n<h4>The Orchestrator</h4>\n<p>Underneath is a small state graph engine with the pieces that matter in production agent frameworks: named nodes over a shared state, fixed and conditional edges, per node retries, a max step guard against infinite loops, and a complete execution trace. It uses no agent framework on purpose, and porting the same graph to a library like LangGraph is a mechanical change. Every tool call is recorded, so a run produces an auditable trace of exactly what the agent did.</p>\n\n<h4>The LLM</h4>\n<p>The agent depends only on a small LLM interface. The default behind it is a deterministic mock model, which keeps runs and tests reproducible offline. Swapping in a hosted model is a one method change with no edits elsewhere, because the pipeline depends on the interface, not the provider.</p>\n\n<h4>Honest Limitations</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Synthetic sample.</strong> It runs on the same fictional brand corpus as media-pulse.</li>\n<li><strong>Mock model.</strong> The default LLM templates the report wording; a hosted model improves the phrasing, not the pipeline.</li>\n<li><strong>Simulated reviewer.</strong> In the demo the human review step is backed by an oracle, where production would use a real review UI.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<div class='callout'>\n<h4>What This Project Demonstrates</h4>\n<p style='margin-bottom: 0;'>Agent orchestration built without a framework: a stateful graph with planning, tool calling, conditional routing, retries, and a human in the loop gate, wrapped around a real task and producing an auditable trace. The model handles the confident mentions, and a human handles the uncertain tail before anything reaches the report.</p>\n</div>"}, {"title": "Project Mark: Designing Deterministic Analytics Traps to Stress Test Frontier Models", "meta": "AI Evaluation &middot; Analytics Red Teaming &middot; Jul 2026", "badge": "4 verified statistical traps &middot; frontier-model evaluation", "tags": ["Python", "Pandas", "NumPy", "SciPy", "Statistical Inference", "Causal Reasoning", "Experiment Design", "LLM Evaluation", "Data Cleaning", "Reproducibility"], "body": "<div class=\"metric\">5 datasets profiled &nbsp;|&nbsp; 4 trap families built and verified &nbsp;|&nbsp; deterministic golden solutions reproduced to the digit &nbsp;|&nbsp; CC licensed multi file corpora &nbsp;|&nbsp; frontier models solved the clean traps</div>\n\n          <h4>What The Project Was</h4>\n          <p>\n            The brief was unusually specific. Every submission had to be a product analytics question of the kind a technology company actually faces, analyzing how users behave inside a digital product to drive a product, growth, or monetization decision. Each had to resolve to a single deterministic recommendation that ten domain experts would independently agree on, be reconstructible entirely from the shipped files, and be hard enough that a panel of frontier models would fail it. A valid stump had a concrete bar. Across eight model responses, at least two of the first four and one of the second four had to land on a meaningfully different answer. Difficulty had to come from methodology and reasoning, never from vague wording or a trick definition.\n          </p>\n\n          <h4>The Real Constraint: Fairness Plus Difficulty At The Same Time</h4>\n          <p>\n            A good task has to be fair and hard at once. Fair means the correcting fact lives somewhere in the corpus, every file opens with standard tooling, and all defensible cleanings converge on the same answer. Hard means a strong analyst, or a strong model, still gets it wrong on the first pass. The input files also had to be real and properly licensed (public domain or Creative Commons only, verified per dataset) and never model generated, with six or more files across a variety of formats and several of them load bearing. Satisfying every one of those at once, on real data, is most of the work.\n          </p>\n\n          <h4>Traps I Built And Verified</h4>\n          <p>\n            Every trap below was implemented against real data and checked with code so the intended answer reproduced exactly before anything shipped. Each one pairs a headline number that is real, easy to compute, and misleading with a correcting analysis that a rushed solver skips.\n          </p>\n          <ul>\n            <li><strong>Composition shift (Simpson's paradox).</strong> A blended retention or conversion metric that declines quarter over quarter while every underlying segment is flat or improving, because the mix of segments moved. The naive read (the product is getting worse) is refuted by a segmentation the solver has to discover and join across files on a mismatched key.</li>\n            <li><strong>Outlier artifact in an A/B revenue test.</strong> On a real experiment dataset, one arm's revenue looked competitive, but 123 users (0.06 percent of the group) each paid a near identical anomalous amount and accounted for 89.4 percent of that arm's revenue, while the other arm had none. The correct call requires recognizing the cluster as non genuine, handling the zero inflated heavy tailed distribution properly (decompose conversion times value, use robust tests), and flipping the decision the raw averages imply.</li>\n            <li><strong>Confounded and circular metrics.</strong> A composite health score presented as independent corroboration that actually embeds the very metric under investigation, so citing it double counts one signal. The same family covers correlation dressed up as causation on observational data.</li>\n            <li><strong>Mismatched population merges.</strong> A distractor file with similar looking columns that covers a different population entirely (different platform, window, or cohort), tempting a solver to merge it in and report a figure that does not apply to the group in question.</li>\n          </ul>\n\n          <h4>The Final Task: An Engagement Feature Decision On Real Learning Platform Data</h4>\n          <p>\n            The most fully developed task used the Open University Learning Analytics Dataset (real virtual learning environment click logs, registrations, and outcomes for tens of thousands of students, released under CC BY). The business question was which one engagement initiative to fund to raise course pass rates: a richer per session experience, or a nudge to return on more days. A shipped internal pre read recommended the per session option, backed by a real and reproducible correlation (total clicks versus passing at r = 0.56, with pass rate climbing from 0.3 percent to 91.9 percent across activity quintiles).\n          </p>\n          <p>\n            That correlation is the trap. Total activity confounds two distinct levers: how many days a student returns, and how much they do per session. Decompose clicks into active days times clicks per active day, regress both against passing, and the entire association lives in return frequency (standardized effect around 0.24, and about 0.34 with all students included). Per session intensity collapses to essentially zero once days present is controlled. The result held within every module and whether or not withdrawn students were included, so the deterministic answer is to fund return frequency, not intensity. I built two corroborating decoy artifacts pointing at the wrong lever and verified that every figure reproduced from the raw files. The task cleared the program's automated construction and determinism checks.\n          </p>\n\n          <h4>Why This Is A Happy Failure</h4>\n          <p>\n            It did not stump the models. In repeated live rollouts, strong frontier models independently rebuilt the analysis from the raw files, ran the frequency versus intensity decomposition, ignored the decoy pre reads, and reached the correct answer, often quoting the exact adjusted numbers. Hardening the framing and adding more authoritative decoys did not change the outcome. The reason is structural. The move that cracks the task, decompose a composite metric and test the parts, is a reflexive step for a capable analyst, so a frontier model does it whether or not the prompt signposts it.\n          </p>\n          <p>\n            Stumping a frontier model on a clean, fair analytics problem requires a trap that sits outside a strong reasoner's default repertoire, which is a far higher bar than \"a smart person would need to be careful here.\" I watched capable models reject a plausible but confounded headline artifact and reconstruct the correct causal story from first principles. That is a calibrated data point about where these systems are now strong, and it changed how I think about designing evaluations for them.\n          </p>\n\n          <div class=\"callout\">\n            <h4>What This Project Demonstrates</h4>\n            <p style=\"margin-bottom: 0;\">\n              I built adversarial, deterministic traps (Simpson's paradox, outlier and confounding artifacts, correlation versus causation, population mismatches) on real, licensed, multi file data and verified each one reproducibly. Trying, and failing, to beat frontier models fairly is what gave me a calibrated read on their capability. The less visible work was disciplined data provenance and licensing, golden solutions checked to the digit before shipping, and reporting an honest negative result.\n            </p>\n          </div>"}, {"title": "media-pulse: Communications Measurement with Human-in-the-Loop QA", "meta": "Applied NLP &middot; Human in the Loop &middot; Aug 2026", "badge": "topic acc 0.88 &rarr; 0.96 with review &middot; sentiment 0.50 &rarr; 0.85 &middot; ECE 0.156", "tags": ["Python", "Scikit learn", "VADER", "TF-IDF", "Logistic Regression", "Streamlit", "Calibration (ECE)", "Human in the Loop", "Responsible AI"], "body": "<div class='metric'>120 sample mentions &middot; topic accuracy 0.88 &rarr; 0.96 after review &middot; sentiment 0.50 &rarr; 0.85 &middot; topic ECE 0.156 &middot; held out test n = 48</div>\n\n<h4>Why This Matters</h4>\n<p>media-pulse runs the full communications measurement loop in one system, ingestion through reporting, rather than a single model in a notebook. It covers the mix that communications measurement roles ask for at once: AI classification paired with human quality assurance, media and sentiment analytics, internal tooling for non technical teams, and responsible AI practice.</p>\n\n<h4>What It Does</h4>\n<p>Ingestion is pluggable: a bundled sample source ships with the project and an RSS adapter shows how a live feed drops into the same schema without changing anything downstream. Each mention is scored for sentiment (VADER) and topic (a TF-IDF and logistic regression model), with a confidence attached to every prediction. It also gets message pull-through detection for pre approved key messages and a relevance score.</p>\n\n<h4>Human in the Loop</h4>\n<p>Any prediction whose confidence falls below a threshold goes to a review queue instead of being trusted. A reviewer confirms or corrects it, the corrected label overwrites the model output, and every action is written to an immutable audit log. On the sample corpus this lifts held out topic accuracy from 0.88 to 0.96 and sentiment accuracy from 0.50 to 0.85, and the dashboard shows the uplift directly. Large citizen science classification pipelines use the same pattern: the model does the bulk of the work and humans adjudicate the uncertain tail.</p>\n\n<h4>Responsible AI</h4>\n<p>Alongside the business metrics (coverage over time, sentiment mix, share of voice weighted by outlet reach, message pull-through), the system runs a quality review of the classifier itself: accuracy on a held out test set, expected calibration error to check that the confidence scores are honest, and an accuracy breakdown by outlet type that shows where the model is weakest.</p>\n\n<h4>Runs Anywhere</h4>\n<p>A four tab Streamlit dashboard (overview, coverage, review queue, model QA) sits on top. The project falls back to bundled numpy and lexicon models when scikit-learn or VADER are not installed, so it runs offline and switches to the production models automatically once the requirements are in place.</p>\n\n<h4>Honest Limitations</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Synthetic corpus.</strong> The data is a deterministic sample about a fictional brand, built to demonstrate the pipeline, not a live media feed.</li>\n<li><strong>Fallback models are weaker.</strong> The lexicon sentiment model used offline is less accurate than a hosted model, which is part of why the human review uplift is large.</li>\n<li><strong>Single node.</strong> It runs on one machine, not at production stream volume.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<div class='callout'>\n<h4>What This Project Demonstrates</h4>\n<p style='margin-bottom: 0;'>One repository covers the qualifications that appear together in communications and analytics roles: AI driven classification with human in the loop QA, media and sentiment measurement, an internal facing dashboard, an end to end data pipeline, and responsible AI practice through calibration, subgroup review by outlet type, and a full audit trail.</p>\n</div>"}, {"title": "Multi Horizon Geomagnetic Kp Index Prediction", "meta": "Deep Learning &middot; 2026", "badge": "45% better than baseline &middot; 96K+ hourly obs", "tags": ["LSTM", "TensorFlow", "Keras", "Time Series", "NASA OMNI2", "Multi Output", "Regularization"], "body": "<div class=\"metric\">1h RMSE = 0.738 (45% better than baseline) &nbsp;|&nbsp; 1h MAE = 0.476 &nbsp;|&nbsp; 1h R&sup2; = 0.690 &nbsp;|&nbsp; 96,000+ hourly observations</div>\n\n          <h4>Why This Matters</h4>\n          <p>\n            Geomagnetic storms come from solar wind disturbances interacting with Earth's magnetosphere, and they have already done real damage. The 1989 Quebec blackout cut power to six million people for nine hours. The 2003 Halloween storms damaged satellites and disrupted aviation. The economic exposure of severe storms is in the billions of dollars. Operators need lead time, ideally six to twelve hours, to safe satellites, derate transformers, and reroute aviation away from polar GPS dead zones. The Kp index (a 0 to 9 quasi logarithmic scale measured every three hours) is the standard operational trigger. A better Kp forecast at multiple horizons gives operators more lead time to act.\n          </p>\n\n          <h4>Stakeholders and Why Multi Horizon Matters</h4>\n          <p>\n            Different operators need different lead times. Satellite mission controllers need 6 to 12 hour notice to plan orbital maneuvers and power down sensitive instruments. National grid operators need a few hours to derate transformers ahead of geomagnetically induced currents. GPS dependent industries (aviation, surveying, autonomous vehicles, precision agriculture) act on shorter horizons. So the model predicts all four horizons in one forward pass instead of training four separate models. Four separate forecasts might silently disagree with each other; one forward pass gives a single, internally consistent forecast across the full operational range.\n          </p>\n\n          <h4>Data</h4>\n          <p>\n            The data comes from NASA's OMNI2 high resolution database, which holds hourly measurements of solar wind and interplanetary magnetic field parameters. It covers eleven years (January 2013 to December 2024), 96,552 hourly records in total. The window spans the declining phase of solar cycle 24, the 2019 to 2020 solar minimum, and the rising phase of solar cycle 25, so the model sees both quiet and active regimes.\n          </p>\n          <p>\n            From the original 55 parameters I selected 15 features, based on physics literature and correlation analysis against Kp. Feature importance fell into clear physical categories:\n          </p>\n\n          <figure class=\"project-figure\">\n            <img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" src=\"images/aurora_-000.jpg\" alt=\"Pearson correlation of 15 selected features with Kp, grouped by category\">\n            <figcaption>Figure 1. Pearson correlation of selected features with Kp, grouped by IMF, solar wind, derived, and geomagnetic indices</figcaption>\n          </figure>\n\n          <p>\n            The strongest predictor was ap_Index (r = 0.825), followed by auroral electrojet indices (AE r = 0.732, AL r = -0.663, AU r = 0.690). IMF_Bz_GSM correlated more weakly than expected (r = -0.238), because the dataset includes all periods rather than just storm windows.\n          </p>\n\n          <h4>Architecture</h4>\n          <p>\n            The model is a two layer LSTM:\n          </p>\n          <table>\n            <thead>\n              <tr><th>Parameter</th><th>Value</th></tr>\n            </thead>\n            <tbody>\n              <tr><td>Input sequence length</td><td>24 hours (24 timesteps &times; 15 features)</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>Output</td><td>4 predictions (Kp_1h, Kp_3h, Kp_6h, Kp_12h)</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>Loss function</td><td>Mean Squared Error</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>Optimizer</td><td>Adam, initial learning rate 0.001</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>Batch size</td><td>64 sequences</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>Epochs</td><td>Maximum 50, early stopping at epoch 39</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>Normalization</td><td>StandardScaler on all features</td></tr>\n            </tbody>\n          </table>\n\n          <p>\n            The split is chronological (50% train / 16.6% validation / 16.6% test), with 8,761 hour temporal gaps between sets to prevent leakage, because in a time series the sequential boundary matters. Training converged cleanly, with the best validation loss at epoch 39. After that, validation loss plateaued while training loss kept improving (the textbook overfit signature, which early stopping caught).\n          </p>\n\n          <figure class=\"project-figure\">\n            <img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" src=\"images/aurora_-001.jpg\" alt=\"Training and validation loss curves over 49 epochs\">\n            <figcaption>Figure 2. Training and validation loss over epochs, with best epoch 39 marked</figcaption>\n          </figure>\n\n          <h4>Results by Horizon</h4>\n\n          <table>\n            <thead>\n              <tr><th>Horizon</th><th>RMSE</th><th>MAE</th><th>R&sup2;</th><th>Baseline RMSE</th><th>Improvement</th></tr>\n            </thead>\n            <tbody>\n              <tr class=\"winner\"><td><strong>1 hour</strong></td><td><strong>0.738</strong></td><td><strong>0.476</strong></td><td><strong>0.690</strong></td><td>1.34</td><td><strong>+44.9%</strong></td></tr>\n              <tr><td>3 hours</td><td>0.910</td><td>0.655</td><td>0.528</td><td>1.34</td><td>+32.1%</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>6 hours</td><td>1.073</td><td>0.793</td><td>0.343</td><td>1.34</td><td>+19.9%</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>12 hours</td><td>1.207</td><td>0.906</td><td>0.170</td><td>1.34</td><td>+9.9%</td></tr>\n            </tbody>\n          </table>\n\n          <figure class=\"project-figure\">\n            <img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" src=\"images/aurora_-002.jpg\" alt=\"LSTM RMSE vs mean baseline RMSE for all four horizons\">\n            <figcaption>Figure 3. RMSE of the LSTM vs the mean baseline across 1, 3, 6, and 12 hour horizons</figcaption>\n          </figure>\n\n          <p>\n            Performance degrades smoothly with horizon, as it should for any forecasting system, since the signal to noise ratio falls with lead time. The 1 hour horizon is the most useful for short term tactical actions (GPS protection, immediate satellite ops). The 6 and 12 hour horizons carry less confidence but still support strategic planning.\n          </p>\n\n          <h4>Behavior During Real Storm Events</h4>\n          <p>\n            The most useful diagnostic is whether the model can flag a storm coming, even imperfectly. Below is a 200 hour window from the test set covering a real storm event (red shaded zone, where actual Kp crosses the threshold of 5). All four horizons capture the storm onset, but with different lag and magnitude. The 1 hour forecast tracks actual Kp closely; the 12 hour forecast smooths the peak but still indicates elevated activity.\n          </p>\n\n          <figure class=\"project-figure\">\n            <img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" src=\"images/aurora_-003.jpg\" alt=\"Multi-horizon LSTM predictions vs actual Kp over a 200 hour test window\">\n            <figcaption>Figure 4. Multi horizon LSTM predictions vs actual Kp over a 200 hour test window, storm period highlighted in red</figcaption>\n          </figure>\n\n          <h4>Avoiding the Data Leakage Trap</h4>\n          <p>\n            Geomagnetic indices like ap, AE, and AL are strongly correlated with Kp because they all derive from magnetometer measurements responding to the same underlying phenomena. That is a real leakage risk if the model accidentally uses concurrent index values to predict Kp. I verified that the indices are 3 hour averaged values (not instantaneous), that the 24 hour lookback window uses strictly historical values to predict future Kp, and that the 1 year temporal gaps between train, validation, and test sets prevent any contamination across the splits. Operational forecasting literature confirms that including these indices is standard practice rather than a methodological shortcut.\n          </p>\n\n          <div class=\"callout\">\n            <h4>Operational Reading</h4>\n            <p style=\"margin-bottom: 0;\">\n              The 1 hour forecast clears a useful operational bar: 45% better RMSE than naive mean prediction, and R&sup2; of 0.690 (the model explains roughly two thirds of variance in storm intensity). At longer horizons the model degrades but stays informative. One model gives the immediate alarm and a softer signal further out, instead of four separate models that might silently disagree.\n            </p>\n          </div>"}, {"title": "The Perfect Lap: F1 Pit Stop Strategy Optimization", "meta": "Machine Learning &middot; Jul 2025 to Dec 2025", "badge": "2.78-lap MAE &middot; 250K+ records &middot; Streamlit deployed", "tags": ["Random Forest", "Python", "Scikit learn", "Streamlit", "SHAP", "Pandas", "CatBoost", "Extra Trees"], "body": "<div class=\"metric\">Test MAE = 2.78 laps &nbsp;|&nbsp; Test R&sup2; = 0.591 &nbsp;|&nbsp; 250,000+ records &nbsp;|&nbsp; 45 engineered features &nbsp;|&nbsp; Streamlit deployed</div>\n\n          <h4>Why This Matters</h4>\n          <p>\n            Few calls in motorsport carry more weight than pit stop timing. A miscall on lap selection can cost a team between two and five constructor championship positions over a season, which means millions of dollars in lost prize money and sponsorship value. Race engineers make these calls in the moment, with limited time, from experience and real time telemetry. The premise of this project was that if a model can predict the optimal lap accurately enough, it becomes a live sanity check that the human strategist either validates or knowingly overrides, instead of a guess they make alone.\n          </p>\n\n          <h4>Stakeholders and Value</h4>\n          <ul>\n            <li><strong>Race engineers</strong> get a predicted first pit lap on race day, a baseline they can use to justify or override their intuition.</li>\n            <li><strong>Strategy directors</strong> get patterns across decades of races for pre season planning, such as circuit and race length outweighing driver identity.</li>\n            <li><strong>Broadcast analysts and fantasy F1 platforms</strong> get a predicted pit lap, and the features that drove it, to explain to viewers.</li>\n          </ul>\n\n          <h4>Data and Cleaning</h4>\n          <p>\n            The source is the Formula 1 World Championship dataset on Kaggle (1950 to 2020), with 250,000+ records across thirteen interconnected tables (races, results, pit stops, drivers, constructors, lap times, qualifying, status codes, circuits, and more). I added a custom <code>track_features</code> table with tire degradation level and pit lane time loss per circuit, because these physical factors drive strategy and the raw schedule data does not capture them well. The data was filtered to post 2000 races to focus on the modern tire era, since pre 2000 strategy was driven by fuel weight rather than tire compounds. The final modeling dataset is 14,000+ driver race rows with 45 engineered features.\n          </p>\n          <p>\n            Cleaning removed invalid pit lap records (below lap 1 or above lap 40 are data entry errors), stripped drivers with more than two pit stops per race (these signal damage or weather chaos rather than normal strategy), and dropped early DNFs so the training set reflects strategic behavior rather than mechanical failures.\n          </p>\n\n          <h4>Recovering Missing Data</h4>\n          <p>\n            Pit records before 2012 were sparse. Dropping every row with a missing pit lap would have shrunk the dataset from 10,000 rows to 3,000. I trained a dedicated Random Forest imputer that recovered roughly 70% of missing first pit laps using six predictors that are reliably present even in older races: driver ID, constructor ID, race year, starting grid position, total race laps, and circuit ID. Imputation MAE was about six laps on a held out validation set, which is tolerable given pit windows typically span ten to fifteen laps.\n          </p>\n\n          <h4>Models Compared</h4>\n          <p>\n            I compared four tree based regressors plus a decision tree baseline, with identical splits and cross validation. I left out neural networks and SVMs on purpose, because they are computationally heavier and consistently underperform tree ensembles on tabular data with this feature mix.\n          </p>\n\n          <table>\n            <thead>\n              <tr><th>Model</th><th>Train MAE</th><th>Test MAE</th><th>Train R&sup2;</th><th>Test R&sup2;</th><th>Status</th></tr>\n            </thead>\n            <tbody>\n              <tr class=\"winner\"><td><strong>Random Forest</strong></td><td>1.007</td><td><strong>2.78</strong></td><td>0.943</td><td><strong>0.591</strong></td><td>Selected</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>Extra Trees</td><td>~0</td><td>2.91</td><td>1.000</td><td>0.551</td><td>Overfits</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>CatBoost</td><td>2.540</td><td>3.02</td><td>0.702</td><td>0.523</td><td>2nd best</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>Gradient Boosting</td><td>3.234</td><td>3.15</td><td>0.564</td><td>0.487</td><td>Weakest</td></tr>\n            </tbody>\n          </table>\n\n          <p>\n            Random Forest reached a Test MAE of 2.78 laps and Test R&sup2; of 0.591, so on average its predicted pit lap is roughly three laps from what teams actually did. Extra Trees showed near perfect training scores but worse generalization, the classic memorization signature. Gradient Boosting struggled with the noise introduced by safety cars and weather changes, since it prefers smoother data patterns. CatBoost would normally have a categorical handling advantage, but our pipeline pre encoded categorical variables for fair comparison, which removed that edge.\n          </p>\n\n          <figure class=\"project-figure\">\n            <img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" src=\"images/f1_01.png\" alt=\"Feature importance comparison across four models\">\n            <figcaption>Figure 1. Feature importance across Random Forest, Extra Trees, Gradient Boosting, and CatBoost</figcaption>\n          </figure>\n\n          <h4>What the Model Learned</h4>\n          <p>\n            SHAP analysis on the Random Forest model shows that race structure features dominate the prediction. The top five contributors are total laps, planned pit stop count, year (proxying for regulation changes), round, and starting grid position. Driver and constructor identities mattered considerably less than F1 commentary tradition suggests.\n          </p>\n\n          <figure class=\"project-figure\">\n            <img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" src=\"images/f1_03.png\" alt=\"SHAP beeswarm plot of feature impact on model output\">\n            <figcaption>Figure 2. SHAP beeswarm showing direction and magnitude of each feature's impact on predicted pit lap</figcaption>\n          </figure>\n\n          <div class=\"callout\">\n            <h4>Practical Implication</h4>\n            <p style=\"margin-bottom: 0;\">\n              Pit timing is more a property of the physical system (circuit, race length, planned stops) than of the person behind the wheel. For engineers, that means adapting strategy to circuit and race length first, team history second, driver style third.\n            </p>\n          </div>\n\n          <h4>Deployment</h4>\n          <p>\n            The final Random Forest model was serialized and packaged into a Streamlit web application (<code>team_omega_rf_app.py</code>). Race engineers input live race context (circuit, grid position, planned stops, weather) and the app returns a predicted first pit stop lap in real time, alongside the SHAP feature importances that drove the prediction. Serialized model artifacts (<code>f1_random_forest_model.pkl</code>, <code>model_features.pkl</code>) live in the project repository.\n          </p>\n\n          <h4>Honest Limitations</h4>\n          <ul>\n            <li><strong>No live race signals.</strong> Safety car deployments, sudden weather changes, and real time tire telemetry are absent. These drive real pit decisions, so there is a genuine information gap between the static model and live race conditions.</li>\n            <li><strong>First stop only.</strong> The model predicts only the first pit lap, not full multi stop strategies. Later stops depend on the first, and the current approach does not capture that dependency.</li>\n            <li><strong>No competitor model.</strong> Real strategy reacts to opponents (undercuts, overcuts). The model encodes neither opponent positions nor gap sizes.</li>\n            <li><strong>Regulation drift.</strong> F1 rules change often. Patterns from early 2000s races may not apply directly to current regulations, which slightly dilutes accuracy when predicting contemporary strategy.</li>\n          </ul>"}, {"title": "Managing July Energy Demand Under a Warming Climate", "meta": "Predictive Modeling &middot; Jan 2025 to May 2025", "badge": "+25.4% peak load at +5&deg;C &middot; 9.2% savings modeled", "tags": ["Random Forest", "XGBoost", "Linear Regression", "Python", "Climate Scenarios", "Residential Energy"], "body": "<div class=\"metric\">Random Forest R&sup2; = 0.48 &nbsp;|&nbsp; RMSE = 0.500 kWh &nbsp;|&nbsp; +25.4% projected peak hour load at +5&deg;C &nbsp;|&nbsp; 9.2% savings from 1&deg;C setpoint shift</div>\n\n          <h4>Why This Matters</h4>\n          <p>\n            When July temperatures rise, residential air conditioning load swings hard upward, peak hour demand spikes, and grid operators face a choice: build more generation (slow, expensive, capital intensive) or shift demand through pricing and behavioral signals (fast, cheap, but only effective if you know what works). This project gives eSC the second tool. We forecast residential consumption under a sustained warming scenario, then quantify which interventions recover the most demand without infrastructure investment.\n          </p>\n\n          <h4>Stakeholders</h4>\n          <ul>\n            <li><strong>Utility capacity planners</strong> get a modeled peak hour load under a sustained warming scenario instead of extrapolating last summer.</li>\n            <li><strong>Demand response program managers</strong> get a savings estimate for the simplest intervention available (a 1&deg;C cooling setpoint shift), which they can use to justify smart thermostat rollouts.</li>\n            <li><strong>Regulators and ratepayer advocates</strong> get evidence on how much behavioral and efficiency programs can substitute for new generation capacity.</li>\n          </ul>\n\n          <h4>Data</h4>\n          <p>\n            Three datasets were joined on common keys: a household metadata file (square footage, occupants, wall insulation type, cooling setpoint), an hourly energy consumption file linked by <code>bldg_id</code>, and a weather file linked by <code>in.county</code>. A metadata file documented what each column means. The combined data was filtered to July to focus on peak cooling season, then merged into one record per house per hour. A reusable merge function lets the same pipeline run on other months and seasons.\n          </p>\n\n          <h4>What Drives Residential Consumption</h4>\n          <p>\n            EDA established four primary drivers of July energy usage:\n          </p>\n\n          <div class=\"figure-row\">\n            <figure class=\"project-figure\">\n              <img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" src=\"images/energy_12.png\" alt=\"Energy usage vs dry bulb temperature\">\n              <figcaption>Figure 1. Energy usage increases with outdoor dry bulb temperature</figcaption>\n            </figure>\n            <figure class=\"project-figure\">\n              <img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" src=\"images/energy_09.png\" alt=\"Energy usage vs square footage\">\n              <figcaption>Figure 2. Energy usage scales near linearly with household square footage</figcaption>\n            </figure>\n          </div>\n\n          <div class=\"figure-row\">\n            <figure class=\"project-figure\">\n              <img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" src=\"images/energy_11.png\" alt=\"Energy usage by number of occupants\">\n              <figcaption>Figure 3. Energy usage by number of occupants</figcaption>\n            </figure>\n            <figure class=\"project-figure\">\n              <img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" src=\"images/energy_10.png\" alt=\"Energy usage by wall insulation type\">\n              <figcaption>Figure 4. Energy usage by wall insulation type</figcaption>\n            </figure>\n          </div>\n\n          <p>\n            Outdoor temperature is the dominant driver, and consumption scales near linearly with square footage. Occupant count matters but plateaus quickly. Usage varies only modestly across wall insulation types in the housing stock, which suggests envelope upgrades have lower marginal return than HVAC and setpoint interventions on this dataset.\n          </p>\n\n          <h4>Models Compared</h4>\n          <p>\n            Three models were trained on five features (dry bulb temperature, square footage, occupants, cooling setpoint, hour of day):\n          </p>\n\n          <table>\n            <thead>\n              <tr><th>Model</th><th>RMSE (kWh)</th><th>R&sup2;</th><th>Notes</th></tr>\n            </thead>\n            <tbody>\n              <tr><td>Linear Regression</td><td>0.583</td><td>0.29</td><td>Cannot capture non linear temperature response</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>XGBoost</td><td>0.503</td><td>0.47</td><td>Strong on non linear patterns, close second</td></tr>\n              <tr class=\"winner\"><td><strong>Random Forest</strong></td><td><strong>0.500</strong></td><td><strong>0.48</strong></td><td>Selected for forecasting</td></tr>\n            </tbody>\n          </table>\n\n          <h4>The +5&deg;C Stress Test</h4>\n          <p>\n            Sustained warming scenarios were applied directly to the temperature feature and pushed through the trained model:\n          </p>\n          <ul>\n            <li><strong>Average July hour, baseline</strong>: 1.09 kWh predicted.</li>\n            <li><strong>Average July hour, +5&deg;C</strong>: 1.25 kWh predicted (a 14.7% increase).</li>\n            <li><strong>Peak hour (9 PM) baseline</strong>: 81,600 kWh aggregate across the sample.</li>\n            <li><strong>Peak hour (9 PM) at +5&deg;C</strong>: 102,355 kWh aggregate, a 25.4% surge.</li>\n          </ul>\n\n          <div class=\"callout\">\n            <h4>Policy Lever: 1&deg;C Setpoint Adjustment</h4>\n            <p>\n              Layering a 1&deg;C cooling setpoint increase during peak hours (16:00 to 21:00) on top of the +5&deg;C warming scenario produces:\n            </p>\n            <ul>\n              <li>Average usage at +5&deg;C: 1.30 kWh per hour</li>\n              <li>Average usage with +1&deg;C setpoint adjustment: 1.18 kWh per hour</li>\n              <li><strong>Average savings: 0.12 kWh per hour, or 9.2% of peak hour demand</strong></li>\n            </ul>\n            <p style=\"margin-bottom: 0;\">\n              A single degree of thermostat tolerance, applied across a customer base, cuts modeled peak hour demand in the warming scenario by nearly one tenth, with no infrastructure investment.\n            </p>\n          </div>\n\n          <h4>Recommendations to eSC</h4>\n          <ul>\n            <li><strong>Time of use pricing during peak hours.</strong> Shift discretionary demand off peak through price signals rather than capacity expansion.</li>\n            <li><strong>Smart thermostat rollout.</strong> Thermostats can apply the 1&deg;C setpoint adjustment automatically across the customer base instead of relying on individual behavior change.</li>\n            <li><strong>Modern HVAC incentives.</strong> Older units carry disproportionate cooling load; replacement programs targeted at the worst performers compound the setpoint benefit.</li>\n            <li><strong>Passive cooling design for new builds.</strong> A long term lever that compounds over the multi decade life of the housing stock.</li>\n          </ul>"}, {"title": "Challenging \"Wealth Equals Health\": Education as the Hidden Lever", "meta": "Quantitative Data Storytelling &middot; Jan 2025 to May 2025", "badge": "180+ countries &middot; ANOVA F = 426.5 (p &lt; 2e-16)", "tags": ["R", "ggplot2", "Multiple Regression", "ANOVA", "Bayesian Inference", "Gapminder"], "body": "<div class=\"metric\">180+ countries &nbsp;|&nbsp; Period: 2000 to 2020 &nbsp;|&nbsp; Combined BF = 1.65 &times; 10&#179;&#x2078;&#x2078; &nbsp;|&nbsp; ANOVA F = 426.5 (p &lt; 2e-16)</div>\n\n          <h4>Why This Matters</h4>\n          <p>\n            \"Richer countries are healthier\" is one of the most common framings in development work, and it has a real consequence for where money goes. Development funding tends to flow toward GDP growth as the master strategy, with health and education treated as downstream beneficiaries. The explicit goal here was evidence a community leader can use to argue for education investment in rural communities. The final report is written for an intentionally non technical audience: adults, teenagers, and children who can act on the conclusion.\n          </p>\n\n          <h4>Research Questions</h4>\n          <ol>\n            <li>To what extent does income influence life expectancy across countries?</li>\n            <li>Can education serve as a more effective tool than income in reducing child mortality?</li>\n          </ol>\n\n          <h4>Data</h4>\n          <p>\n            The data is the Gapminder global dataset, covering 180+ countries between 2000 and 2020, with these variables:\n          </p>\n          <ul>\n            <li><strong>GDP per capita</strong> as the measure of national income, log transformed to address right skew from extreme high income outliers.</li>\n            <li><strong>Life expectancy</strong> as the primary health outcome, ranging from roughly 40 to 85 years.</li>\n            <li><strong>Child mortality</strong> as the most policy responsive health metric, ranging from fewer than 5 deaths per 1,000 births in high income countries to over 150 in low income countries.</li>\n            <li><strong>Mean years of schooling</strong> for adults aged 25 to 34, the most reliable cross country proxy for education access, ranging from 2 to 15 years.</li>\n          </ul>\n\n          <h4>Exploratory Visualizations</h4>\n\n          <div class=\"figure-row\">\n            <figure class=\"project-figure\">\n              <img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" src=\"images/wealth_02.png\" alt=\"GDP vs Life Expectancy scatter plot with regression line\">\n              <figcaption>Figure 1. Log GDP vs life expectancy, all countries 2000 to 2020</figcaption>\n            </figure>\n            <figure class=\"project-figure\">\n              <img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" src=\"images/wealth_04.png\" alt=\"GDP vs Child Mortality scatter plot with regression line\">\n              <figcaption>Figure 2. Log GDP vs child mortality, all countries 2000 to 2020</figcaption>\n            </figure>\n          </div>\n\n          <figure class=\"project-figure\">\n            <img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" src=\"images/wealth_03.png\" alt=\"Education vs Child Mortality scatter plot showing strong negative relationship\">\n            <figcaption>Figure 3. Mean years of schooling vs child mortality. The negative relationship is stronger and tighter than for GDP</figcaption>\n          </figure>\n\n          <h4>Question 1: Does GDP Predict Life Expectancy?</h4>\n          <p>\n            Linear regression of life expectancy on log GDP per capita gave a coefficient of <strong>17.6</strong> (p &lt; 2e-16), a strong positive relationship. Bayesian regression returned a Bayes Factor of <strong>5.66 &times; 10&#178;&#185;&#x2078;</strong> in favor of GDP as a predictor, which is overwhelming evidence. The answer to the first question is yes, GDP matters.\n          </p>\n\n          <h4>Question 2: Does Education Beat GDP for Child Mortality?</h4>\n          <p>\n            Multiple regression of child mortality on both mean years of schooling and log GDP returned two significant negative coefficients:\n          </p>\n          <ul>\n            <li><strong>Mean years of schooling</strong>: estimate = -0.76, p &lt; 2e-16</li>\n            <li><strong>Log GDP per capita</strong>: estimate = -74.14, p &lt; 2e-16</li>\n          </ul>\n          <p>\n            Bayesian model comparison returned a combined Bayes Factor of <strong>1.65 &times; 10&#178;&#x2078;&#x2078;</strong> in favor of the model that includes both predictors. That is overwhelming evidence that each predictor carries independent information about child mortality.\n          </p>\n\n          <h4>Confirmatory Tests</h4>\n          <p>\n            Welch's t test compared child mortality between high education and low education country groups:\n          </p>\n\n          <figure class=\"project-figure\">\n            <img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" src=\"images/wealth_09.png\" alt=\"Boxplot of child mortality by education group\">\n            <figcaption>Figure 4. Child mortality by education group (Welch's t test). Mean difference &#8776; 57 deaths per 1,000 births, p &lt; 2.2e-16</figcaption>\n          </figure>\n\n          <p>\n            One way ANOVA on life expectancy by GDP group (Low, Medium, High) returned F = 426.5, p &lt; 2e-16:\n          </p>\n\n          <figure class=\"project-figure\">\n            <img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" src=\"images/wealth_10.png\" alt=\"Boxplot of life expectancy by GDP group\">\n            <figcaption>Figure 5. Life expectancy by GDP group (ANOVA, F = 426.5, p &lt; 2e-16)</figcaption>\n          </figure>\n\n          <div class=\"callout\">\n            <h4>The Practical Conclusion</h4>\n            <p>\n              Income matters, but it is not the lever development funders should pull first. Education is just as strong a predictor of child mortality as GDP is, and unlike GDP, a rural community can act on it directly through school access, teacher training, and maternal literacy programs.\n            </p>\n            <p style=\"margin-bottom: 0;\">\n              Each additional year of schooling is associated with a measurable drop in child deaths, regardless of country wealth. For a community leader, the evidence based course is to keep children (especially girls) in school through secondary education, combine schooling with practical instruction on hygiene, nutrition, vaccination, and early childhood care, and advocate for school infrastructure investment.\n            </p>\n          </div>"}, {"title": "Uber Data Engineering Pipeline", "meta": "Data Engineering &middot; Jul 2024 to Dec 2024", "badge": "End-to-end GCP pipeline &middot; star schema &middot; BigQuery", "tags": ["GCP", "BigQuery", "Power BI", "SQL", "Python", "ETL", "Star Schema"], "body": "<div class=\"metric\">End to end cloud pipeline &nbsp;|&nbsp; Star schema modeling &nbsp;|&nbsp; Cloud Storage + Compute Engine + BigQuery &nbsp;|&nbsp; Interactive BI dashboard</div>\n\n          <h4>Objective</h4>\n          <p>\n            Most data engineering exercises stop at a notebook. I wanted the full lifecycle in one project, end to end on a real cloud stack: ingestion, storage, transformation, modeling, querying, and visualization. The deliverable was the entire path from a raw data file to a stakeholder ready dashboard, rather than a single model or chart.\n          </p>\n\n          <h4>Architecture</h4>\n          <p>\n            I modeled the raw NYC taxi trip data as a star schema in Lucid Charts, with fact and dimension tables and explicit primary and foreign key relationships. The infrastructure was deployed on Google Cloud Platform in three layers:\n          </p>\n          <ul>\n            <li><strong>Storage layer.</strong> Cloud Storage as the data lake for raw and intermediate Parquet files.</li>\n            <li><strong>Compute layer.</strong> Compute Engine VMs running Python ETL jobs that read from Cloud Storage, transform with Pandas and NumPy, and write back as cleaned Parquet.</li>\n            <li><strong>Analytics layer.</strong> BigQuery for SQL analytics at scale, with the cleaned data loaded as partitioned tables for efficient time range queries.</li>\n          </ul>\n\n          <h4>Analysis and Presentation</h4>\n          <p>\n            Exploratory analysis in Jupyter surfaced patterns in trip distance distribution, fare structure, revenue concentration, and service usage across boroughs and times of day. The findings then fed an interactive Power BI dashboard with geographical heat maps, time series views, and slicers for stakeholder driven exploration.\n          </p>\n\n          <h4>Lesson</h4>\n          <p>\n            Moving from local CSV files to distributed cloud storage and compute changed how I think about data work more than any individual tool did. When data lives in object storage and compute is elastic, scaling up by an order of magnitude becomes a configuration change rather than a bottleneck.\n          </p>"}, {"title": "Travel Itinerary Management System", "meta": "Database Engineering &middot; Jan 2025 to May 2025", "badge": "12-entity normalized schema &middot; full itinerary in one call", "tags": ["SQL", "Data Modeling", "Stored Procedures", "Triggers", "User Defined Functions", "ERD Design", "3NF"], "body": "<div class=\"metric\">12 normalized entities &nbsp;|&nbsp; 3 stored procedures &nbsp;|&nbsp; 2 triggers &nbsp;|&nbsp; 1 user defined function &nbsp;|&nbsp; Single procedure call generates full itinerary</div>\n\n          <h4>Why This Matters</h4>\n          <p>\n            An itinerary database carries rules that have to hold on every access path: an activity cannot have negative cost, a hotel must belong to a real destination, an itinerary cost should always equal the sum of its components. Put those rules in application code and each new feature or platform reintroduces them, badly. This project puts them in the database itself, so any client (web, mobile, batch import, ad hoc SQL) gets the same guarantees automatically.\n          </p>\n\n          <h4>Stakeholders</h4>\n          <ul>\n            <li><strong>Travelers</strong> creating a trip get one call that returns a complete itinerary, with the duration and cost checks already run.</li>\n            <li><strong>Application developers</strong> stop writing defensive code in three languages to enforce rules the database already enforces.</li>\n            <li><strong>Data analysts</strong> querying the database directly get consistent data, because the triggers and foreign keys also apply to rows written through direct SQL.</li>\n          </ul>\n\n          <h4>Entity Relationship Design</h4>\n          <p>\n            The schema has twelve entities in third normal form with full referential integrity. The core entity is <code>itinerary</code>, which links to destinations, activities, accommodations, transportation, seasonal information, and tags through explicit foreign keys.\n          </p>\n\n          <figure class=\"project-figure\">\n            <img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" src=\"images/travel_03.png\" alt=\"Logical ERD showing all 12 tables with primary keys, foreign keys, and relationships\">\n            <figcaption>Figure 1. Logical ERD with all 12 tables, primary keys, foreign keys, and cardinality relationships</figcaption>\n          </figure>\n\n          <h4>Stored Procedures</h4>\n          <p>\n            Three procedures cover the operations a travel app actually performs:\n          </p>\n          <ul>\n            <li><code>sp_add_full_itinerary_single</code>: Inserts a complete itinerary (activity, transport, accommodation, season info, tag) in a single atomic call. It replaces five separate inserts plus consistency checks at the application layer.</li>\n            <li><code>sp_get_full_itinerary</code>: Returns every linked detail of an itinerary by ID, with the joins done inside the database.</li>\n            <li><code>sp_get_itineraries_for_destination</code>: Lists all itineraries available under a selected destination, the most common browse query.</li>\n          </ul>\n\n          <h4>Triggers for Data Integrity</h4>\n          <ul>\n            <li><code>trg_check_activity_duration</code>: Rejects any activity insert or update where duration is zero or negative. That catches UI bugs and data import errors that would otherwise silently corrupt the cost calculations downstream.</li>\n            <li><code>trg_check_activity_cost</code>: Rejects negative cost entries, which usually indicate a typo or unit conversion error rather than a genuine refund.</li>\n          </ul>\n\n          <h4>Function for Reusable Logic</h4>\n          <ul>\n            <li><code>ufn_estimate_total_activity_time</code>: Returns the total time a traveler will spend on all activities in a given itinerary. Views, filters, and ranking interfaces call it, so itineraries can be sorted or filtered by total activity duration without each of them redoing the math.</li>\n          </ul>\n\n          <h4>User Story Worked Through End to End</h4>\n          <p>\n            <em>\"As a traveler, I want to create a trip by selecting options, so I can generate a full itinerary.\"</em>\n          </p>\n          <p>\n            The client passes destination, activity, transport, accommodation, season, and tag to <code>sp_add_full_itinerary_single</code>. The procedure inserts each child record, links them through the itinerary table, fires the duration and cost triggers, and returns the new <code>itinerary_id</code>. The client makes one round trip, and the database validates the whole insert and applies it atomically.\n          </p>\n\n          <div class=\"callout\">\n            <h4>Design Philosophy</h4>\n            <p style=\"margin-bottom: 0;\">\n              With the business rules at the data layer, the application code gets simpler and integrity holds on every read and write path. When the database itself rejects a zero duration or a negative cost, no client has to write defensive code in its own language to catch it.\n            </p>\n          </div>"}, {"title": "Digit Recognition Neural Network from Scratch", "meta": "Deep Learning &middot; Jan 2024 to May 2024", "badge": "94% MNIST accuracy &middot; built from scratch, no frameworks", "tags": ["Python", "NumPy", "Neural Networks", "Backpropagation", "From Scratch"], "body": "<div class=\"metric\">94% test accuracy on MNIST &nbsp;|&nbsp; No ML frameworks &nbsp;|&nbsp; Manual forward and backward passes</div>\n\n          <h4>Why Build from Scratch</h4>\n          <p>\n            Frameworks hide the parts of a neural network that are most useful to understand: the matrix shapes flowing forward, the gradient math flowing backward, and the way optimization changes the weights. Building all three by hand once gives you a working mental model of what the framework is doing underneath. When a production model later silently fails, you reason about gradient flow rather than swapping layers and hoping.\n          </p>\n\n          <h4>Implementation</h4>\n          <p>\n            Forward propagation is explicit weighted sums and activation functions: sigmoid in the hidden layers in the first iteration, then ReLU once vanishing gradients became visible, and softmax at the output for the ten class classification. Backpropagation computes gradients with the chain rule, layer by layer, using an explicit derivative formula for each activation function. Training runs in a mini batch gradient descent loop with tunable learning rate, batch size, and network depth.\n          </p>\n\n          <h4>Tuning</h4>\n          <p>\n            Hyperparameter tuning was iterative and manual, which was the point. With the learning rate too high the loss bounced, and with it too low, training stalled. A network that was too shallow could not capture digit structure, and in one that was too deep without proper initialization the gradients vanished. Working through these failures by hand is how you learn what each knob controls.\n          </p>\n\n          <h4>Result</h4>\n          <p>\n            Final accuracy was 94% on the held out MNIST test set. That is not state of the art; the goal was understanding rather than benchmark chasing. The exercise made it intuitive why deeper networks, non linear activations, and proper initialization matter.\n          </p>"}, {"title": "Real Time Big Data Inflow: Communication and Computation Delay Analysis", "meta": "Research &middot; Jan 2024 to Dec 2024", "badge": "40 to 60% latency reduction &middot; 6-dimension comparison", "tags": ["IoT", "Edge Computing", "Fog Computing", "Cloud Computing", "Stream Architecture"], "body": "<div class=\"metric\">40 to 60% latency reduction potential &nbsp;|&nbsp; 12+ primary sources reviewed &nbsp;|&nbsp; Comparative analysis across six dimensions</div>\n\n          <h4>Argument</h4>\n          <p>\n            The implicit default in most enterprise architectures is to send everything to the cloud. For real time systems, that default is wrong. The hard design work is deciding which computations belong near the sensor (where latency is cheap but durability is fragile and storage is limited) and which belong in the data center (where storage is durable and compute is elastic but round trips cost real time).\n          </p>\n\n          <h4>Approach</h4>\n          <p>\n            The paper reviews twelve primary sources on stream processing, edge deployment patterns, and latency optimization in distributed systems. Each source is summarized for its proposed architecture and headline findings. From the literature, I propose a tiered hybrid architecture that pushes time critical computation toward the edge and keeps cloud tiers for durability, batch analytics, and global state.\n          </p>\n\n          <h4>Comparative Analysis</h4>\n          <p>\n            The paper compares cloud only and tiered architectures across six dimensions: scalability, reliability, bandwidth usage, privacy and security, cost effectiveness, and end to end latency. The tiered architecture wins on latency (40 to 60% reduction for appropriately partitioned workloads), bandwidth (less raw data crosses the WAN), and privacy (sensitive data can be processed locally). It pays a small price in operational complexity and consistency guarantees.\n          </p>"}];
